== basename (6 cases) ==
  PASS no slash at all: the string is its own basename -- the last pathname component of a name with no slash is the name itself
  PASS trailing slashes are stripped first -- the trailing <slash> characters in the string shall be removed
  PASS a matching suffix is removed -- if the suffix operand is present ... and is not identical to the characters remaining in string ... the suffix ... shall be removed
  PASS a suffix identical to the whole base is not removed -- suffix ... is not identical to the characters remaining in string
  PASS a suffix at least as long as the base is not removed -- the suffix ... shall be removed
  PASS a string of nothing but slashes is "/" -- if string is '//', it is implementation-defined whether steps 2 to 5 are skipped or... string is set to '/'

== cal (6 cases) ==
  PASS a month with 5 body weeks pads a 6th blank row -- cal [[month] year] ... a calendar for the specified month
       hand-authored: no host is the oracle on the box: Debian ships no cal at all, and util-linux/ncal cal lay the month out differently (no 22-column pad, no 6th blank row); the XSI page says only 'a calendar'. Lines captured verbatim from this Mac's own /usr/bin/cal (BSD), which cal.s was measured against.
  PASS February in a leap year has 29 days -- cal ... Gregorian calendar
       hand-authored: no host is the oracle on the box: Debian ships no cal at all, and util-linux/ncal cal lay the month out differently (no 22-column pad, no 6th blank row); the XSI page says only 'a calendar'. Lines captured verbatim from this Mac's own /usr/bin/cal (BSD), which cal.s was measured against.
  PASS a month starting on Sunday has no leading blanks -- cal ... a calendar for the specified month
       hand-authored: no host is the oracle on the box: Debian ships no cal at all, and util-linux/ncal cal lay the month out differently (no 22-column pad, no 6th blank row); the XSI page says only 'a calendar'. Lines captured verbatim from this Mac's own /usr/bin/cal (BSD), which cal.s was measured against.
  PASS December, and a month needing exactly 6 body weeks -- cal ... a calendar for the specified month
       hand-authored: no host is the oracle on the box: Debian ships no cal at all, and util-linux/ncal cal lay the month out differently (no 22-column pad, no 6th blank row); the XSI page says only 'a calendar'. Lines captured verbatim from this Mac's own /usr/bin/cal (BSD), which cal.s was measured against.
  PASS a month outside 1-12 is a usage error -- cal [[month] year]
       hand-authored: month must be 1-12 -- cal.s's own usage path, exit 2 by house convention. No common host row: this host's own cal reads two bare numeric operands as YEAR-only followed by a usage error of its own, a different construct.
  PASS the full-year 3-column BSD layout -- cal [[month] year] ... a calendar for the specified year
       hand-authored: no host is the oracle on the box: Debian ships no cal at all, and util-linux/ncal cal lay the month out differently (no 22-column pad, no 6th blank row); the XSI page says only 'a calendar'. Lines captured verbatim from this Mac's own /usr/bin/cal (BSD), which cal.s was measured against.

== cat (7 cases) ==
  PASS basic single file -- The cat utility shall read files in sequence and shall write their contents to the standard output in the same sequence.
  PASS multiple files concatenated -- The cat utility shall read files in sequence and shall write their contents to the standard output in the same sequence.
  PASS no operands reads stdin -- If no file operands are specified, the standard input shall be used.
  PASS missing file continues, exit>0 -- >0 An error occurred.
  PASS -u is accepted (output is already unbuffered) -- -u Write bytes from the input file to the standard output without delay as each is read.
  PASS '-' means stdin -- If a file is '-', the cat utility shall read from the standard input at that point in the sequence.
  PASS a typed ^C ends cat reading the terminal: status 130 -- EXIT STATUS: the process was terminated by SIGINT -- the shell reports 128+2. If no file operands are specified, the standard input shall be used.

== cksum (4 cases) ==
  PASS prints the CRC, the octet count, and (with an operand) the pathname -- "%d %d %s\n", <CRC value>, <number of octets>, <pathname>
       hand-authored: hand-computed over hilines(['abc']) = $E1 $E2 $E3 $8D (4 bytes) -- 'abc' high-bit ASCII plus its own line terminator; the pathname is the bare relative name, since command_line() cd's into the case's own base directory first and types every operand as the SAME bare name used on the host side (host_mapping()), never a full /mnt/MAUX/PXUT/<K-tag>/... path.
  PASS no operand: read standard input, no pathname column -- If no file operands are specified ... the standard input shall be used ... the pathname shall not be written.
       hand-authored: same 4-byte hilines(['abc']) content as the row above, via stdin instead of an operand -- no NAME column, sum.s's own NAMEFLG shape.
  PASS multiple operands: each gets its own line and pathname, in argument order -- For each file operand ... cksum shall write the CRC and the octet count of the file, and the pathname.
       hand-authored: hand-computed over hilines(['x']) = $F8 $8D and hilines(['y']) = $F9 $8D -- 2 bytes each (bare relative names -- see the row above's own note). Unlike sum.s, cksum names EVERY operand, even a single one (the row above), not only when there is more than one.
  PASS an unopenable operand does not stop the remaining ones -- >0 An error occurred.
       hand-authored: NOSUCH's diagnostic goes to stderr, never compared here (see this file's own module docstring); the SURVIVING operand's line is hand-computed the same way as the two-file row above (hilines(['y']), bare relative name), and the overall exit is 1, sum.s's/cut.s's own 'skip and continue' shape.

== cmp (7 cases) ==
  PASS identical files -- 0 The files are identical.
  PASS default differ message -- "%s %s differ: char %d, line %d\n", file1, file2, <byte number>, <line number>
  PASS -l lists every differing byte pair -- "%d %o %o\n", <byte number>, <differing byte>, <differing byte>
       hand-authored: -l reports the RAW STORED byte value, and the two sides store text in different encodings by design: maux's own text-file convention is high-bit-set ASCII (hi()/hilines(), the same helper check_posixsed.py uses), while this harness's host-side fixture file is plain low-bit ASCII. A live BSD cmp -l on the low-bit pair reports '     3 143 144' (0143 ='c', 0144='d'); maux's cmp reads its own high-bit-set copies of the same two letters and correctly reports '     3 343 344' (0343 = 0143+0200, 0344 = 0144+0200 -- exactly the high bit). Not a maux bug: it is a byte-exact encoding fact about how maux stores text, which -l's raw octal dump exposes and a line-oriented tool's CR/high-bit translation (maux_lines()) would normally hide. Expected value is the host's own octal digits with 0200 added to each, not a captured maux run.
  PASS -s is silent, status only -- Write nothing to standard output or standard error when files differ; indicate differing files through exit status only.
  PASS EOF on the shorter file -- "cmp: EOF on %s%s\n", <name of shorter file>, <additional info>
  PASS missing file operand is an error -- >1 An error occurred.
  PASS -l with -s is refused -- -s Write nothing to standard output or standard error when files differ...

== comm (7 cases) ==
  PASS default: three columns -- produce three text columns as output: lines only in file1, lines only in file2, and lines in both files.
  PASS -3 suppresses the common column -- If the -3 option is not selected, lines contained in both files shall be written...
  PASS -123 writes nothing -- If the -1, -2, and -3 options are all selected, comm shall write nothing to standard output.
  PASS -1 suppresses column 1 (adaptive lead) -- null string -- The -1 option is selected. [column 2's lead]
  PASS -2 suppresses column 2 (adaptive lead) -- <tab> -- Exactly one of the -1 and -2 options is selected. [column 3's lead]
  PASS -12 leaves only the common column -- null string -- Both the -1 and -2 options are selected. [column 3's lead]
  PASS exactly two operands required -- The following operands shall be supported: file1 ... file2
       hand-authored: POSIX's EXIT STATUS clause for comm is the generic '>0 An error occurred.', not a fixed number; BSD comm (this Mac's oracle) happens to pick 1 for a wrong operand count (verified live: 'usage: comm [-123i] file1 file2' on stderr, rc=1), while comm.s's own header documents exit 2 for every usage/bad-option error, matching cmp.s's own '"must be exactly 2" shape' and every other util in this tree. Both are POSIX-legal; this row used to be a KNOWN_GAP for an unrelated reason (the usage message's missing trailing CR made the harness's own prompt-detection time out, not a real hang -- fixed 2026-09-13, module docstring bug 3), and retiring it surfaced this separate, legitimate exit-code divergence that the hang had been masking.

== cp (4 cases) ==
  PASS cp -r copies a directory tree -- Copy file hierarchies.
       hand-authored: the assertion is a filesystem side effect (a whole tree really landed at DEST) proved by a follow-up CAT in the same shell session -- oracle()'s host path runs exactly one process per case and has no `post` hook to diff against, so this is hand-authored to the harness's own known fixture content, not a captured maux run.
  PASS -p preserves ProDOS attributes and dates -- Duplicate... the time of last data modification... the file permission bits
       hand-authored: the harness reads both ProDOS directory entries after cp and compares access, type, aux, creation, and modification fields.
  PASS -i accepts a new destination without prompting -- Write a prompt to standard error before copying to any existing non-directory destination file.
  PASS -i affirmative response replaces destination -- If the response is affirmative, cp shall perform the copy.
       hand-authored: the follow-up CAT proves the affirmative response permitted replacement.

== csplit (9 cases) ==
  PASS a line-number argument splits before that line -- line_number ... Create ... up to but not including line_number.
       hand-authored: hand-computed over hilines(['aaa','bbb']) = 8 B and hilines(['ccc','ddd','eee','fff']) = 16 B; verified live against /usr/bin/csplit (same split point, same two pieces).
  PASS /bre/ splits at the first line matching bre; the matched line starts the NEXT piece -- /bre/[offset] ... the line ... shall become the first line of the next section
       hand-authored: hand-computed hilines() byte counts, 16 and 8; verified live against /usr/bin/csplit -- the matched line ('eee') is NOT in xx00, confirmed by running the host binary before writing csplit.s's RUNSEG (its first cut, before this row, put the match in the WRONG piece -- fixed to match this).
  PASS /bre/+N shifts the cut N lines past the match -- /bre/+offset ... the offset ... from the line ... addressed
       hand-authored: hand-computed hilines() byte counts, 12 and 12; verified live against /usr/bin/csplit: +1 moves the cut one line past the plain (offset 0) point, so 'ccc' now ends xx00.
  PASS %bre% discards the span up to the match -- no piece file for it -- %bre%[offset] ... does not create a file for the selected section
       hand-authored: hand-computed hilines() byte count, 16; verified live against /usr/bin/csplit -- only ONE piece exists (the discarded 'aaa','bbb' span never became a file, and 'xx01' does not exist).
  PASS {n} repeats the previous /bre/ n MORE times -- {n} ... Repeat the previous pattern n additional times.
       hand-authored: hand-computed hilines() byte counts (6 B/line here); verified live against /usr/bin/csplit -- each repeat's own held match line (e.g. 'MARK1') heads its OWN new piece rather than being re-tested and held again (csplitrn.s's own REPMODE, added after this row's live run first showed 4 pieces where an untested guess predicted an empty one).
  PASS -f/-n set the piece prefix and digit width -- -f prefix ... -n number ... the number of decimal digits
       hand-authored: hand-computed hilines() byte counts, 8 and 16; verified live against /usr/bin/csplit (-f Q -n 3 names the pieces Q000/Q001).
  PASS -s suppresses the byte-count stdout -- -s ... Suppress ... the size ... of output files.
  PASS a regex that never matches is an error; pieces removed by default -- If no line matches ... write a diagnostic message ... and exit
       hand-authored: csplit.s's own REGEXERR/ERRCLEANUP path: 'zzz' never matches before EOF, so the partial piece csplitrn.s's OPENPIECE already created is DESTROYed and the run exits 1 -- verified live against /usr/bin/csplit (prints nothing to stdout, no xx00 survives, exit 1).
  PASS -k keeps the partial piece on the same error -- -k ... do not remove output files ... created before ... detected an error
       hand-authored: same error as the row above, but -k: verified live against /usr/bin/csplit -- xx00 survives holding every line scanned before EOF (the whole file, since 'zzz' never matched), exit 1.

== cut (12 cases) ==
  PASS -c selects a range of character positions -- first_number-last_number -- Characters or bytes from first_number to last_number in the line shall be selected.
  PASS -c 'N-' selects to the end of the line -- first_number-  -- ... to the end of the line.
  PASS -c '-M' defaults the low end to the first position -- -last_number -- ... from the first character/byte in the line to last_number.
  PASS the list may repeat and overlap; output stays in line order -- The elements of list can be repeated, can overlap, and can be specified in any order.
  PASS -b is the byte form of the same list syntax -- -b list -- The list following -b specifies byte positions.
  PASS -f default delimiter is <tab> -- -f list -- ... fields ... separated in the input by the delimiter character (see -d) ... The default ... is <tab>.
  PASS -d changes the field delimiter -- -d delim -- Use the character delim as the field delimiter character instead of the <tab> character.
  PASS -s suppresses a line with no delimiter -- -s -- Suppress lines with no delimiter characters.
  PASS without -s a delimiterless line passes through unchanged -- If -s is not specified, ... lines with no delimiter characters shall be written in their entirety.
  PASS missing file continues, exit>0 -- >0 An error occurred.
  PASS exactly one of -b/-c/-f is required: none is a usage error here -- A -b, -c, or -f option must be specified.
       hand-authored: cut.s's START refuses (usage, exit 2) when HAVEMODE is never set -- this project's uniform usage-error convention (grep.s/head.s/etc.), which POSIX's generic wording permits. This host's own /usr/bin/cut also refuses a mode-less invocation, but with its own usage banner on exit 1, not 2 -- the STATUS differs, so this stays hand-authored rather than host-oracled.
  PASS a descending range is a usage error here -- If an element ... consist[s] of two numbers separated by a hyphen ... the first number ... shall be less than the second number.
       hand-authored: cutlist.s's CKORDER refuses a range whose low end exceeds its high end (usage, exit 2) -- the POSIX text quoted above requires first<second. This host's /usr/bin/cut is MORE lenient: measured live, '-c 5-2' exits 0 with an EMPTY selection rather than an error, so this stays hand-authored rather than host-oracled.

== date (2 cases) ==
  PASS no clock: refuses rather than print a fabricated date -- the output... shall be equivalent to specifying: date "+%a %b %e %H:%M:%S %Z %Y" -- which requires an actual reading of the current time
       hand-authored: reset(clock=False) pins the Timemaster card OFF (default ON, mcp__verilogapple__reset's own doc) so SC_CLOCK reads all zero; date.s's own header: 'If the clock is ABSENT... a message goes to fd 2 and it exits 1' -- diagnostic wording is never compared, only stdout (empty: nothing was ever written there) and the exit status. check_sumdate.py already exercises the REAL-clock path with a synchronized peek this batch-oracle harness has no hook for; this proves the courteous-refusal path deterministically instead.
  PASS +format with no clock: still refuses (unchanged) -- When the format is specified, the output shall be formatted as if by strftime()
       hand-authored: SHIPPED 2026-09-17: +FORMAT is now REALLY parsed (PROCFMT/PFDISPATCH, date.s) -- but READCLOCK runs before PROCFMT ever does, and with the clock disabled it refuses exactly the way the no-operand form always has (NOCLOCK, exit 1, nothing on stdout). This row cannot assert the FORMATTED content: check_posixutil's reset(clock=...) is a boolean (present/absent), not a settable wall-clock value, so no batch row here can pin exact +FORMAT output -- that needs check_sumdate.py's own raw-clock-peek mechanism extended to a +FORMAT invocation, not done in this task (date.s's own header names this gap explicitly).

== df (2 cases) ==
  PASS -P prints the POSIX header -- Filesystem 512-blocks Used Available Capacity Mounted on
       hand-authored: the header text is POSIX's own -P format (also what this Mac's `df -P` prints), carried by the `contains` form because the row under it moves with every staged file.
  PASS -P . reports the working directory's volume -- df [-k] [-P] [file...] -- the file system containing each operand.
       hand-authored: `.` resolves through liba's MKPATH to the prefix, whose volume is /mnt/MAUX, 8192 blocks (the 4 MB play carrier); the row begins '/mnt/MAUX 8192 '. Used/Available/Capacity follow it and are check-df's.

== diff (49 cases) ==
  PASS identical files: exit 0, no output -- 0 No differences were found.
  PASS differing files: default ed-script-compatible output -- "%dc%d\n"... "<%s"... "---\n"... ">%s"; 1 Differences were found
  PASS missing file: exit 2 -- >1 An error occurred.
  PASS -e produces a pure ed script -- a script shall be produced that shall, when provided as input to ed... convert file1 into file2
  PASS -u unified context output -- The -u or -U options... the context, deleted, and added lines are shown together, interleaved
  PASS -u merges change regions closer than 2*context -- If the changes are so close that the context lines overlap, the hunks shall be combined
  PASS -U 1 sets the number of context lines -- -U lines -- Produce output in a unified format with lines lines of copied context.
  PASS -u on an empty file1 is a 0,0 range -- If the range consists of no lines, the number printed shall be the line number of the line preceding the range
  PASS -c context output -- Produce output in a form that provides three lines of copied context
  PASS -C 1 sets the number of context lines -- -C lines -- Produce output in a context format with lines lines of copied context.
  PASS -c omits a section with no changed line of its own -- If a hunk contains lines that are only deleted, the second file's section is not written
  PASS -r recursive is unimplemented -- Apply diff recursively to files and directories of the same name when file1 and file2 are both directories
  PASS -b ignores trailing white space -- Cause any amount of white space at the end of a line to be treated as a single <newline>
  PASS -b makes white-space runs compare equal -- Cause any amount of white space at the end of a line to be treated as a single <newline>
  PASS -b does NOT equate a run with no white space -- Cause any amount of white space at the end of a line to be treated as a single <newline>
  PASS -r on two identical trees: exit 0, no output -- 0 No differences were found.
  PASS -r names each differing pair with a diff line -- diff %s %s %s -- for each pair of files that differ
  PASS -r prints Only in for a name in one directory only -- Only in %s: %s -- a file in one directory and not the other
  PASS -r merges the two listings in byte order of the name -- the collating sequence of the current locale -- C: bytes
  PASS -r descends: Only in names the SUBDIRECTORY it is in -- Only in %s: %s -- %s the directory pathname
  PASS -r visits files and subdirectories in one merged order -- recursively ... for each pair of files with the same name
  PASS -r -u: header carries the option as typed -- diff -r -u A B, then the unified listing
  PASS -ru: bundled options are echoed bundled -- the <diff options> are the options as specified
  PASS -r -c: header then the context listing -- diff -r -c A B, then the context listing
  PASS -r -e: header then a pure ed script -- diff -r -e A B, then the ed script
  PASS -r -U 1: an option with an argument echoes both words -- diff -r -U 1 A B
  PASS -r -C 1: context width reaches every pair -- diff -r -C 1 A B
  PASS -r -b: white-space-only differences print nothing -- -b Cause any amount of white space at the end of a line to be treated as a single <newline>
  PASS -rb: -r and -b bundled still both take effect -- diff -rb: the header echoes the bundle, -b hides the diff
  PASS two directories WITHOUT -r: pairs diffed, subdirs named -- Common subdirectories: %s and %s
  PASS two directories WITHOUT -r, only common subdirectories -- Common subdirectories: ... exit 0 when nothing else differs
  PASS FILE DIR compares FILE with DIR/basename, no header -- If only one of file1 and file2 is a directory, diff shall be applied to the non-directory file and the file contained in the directory file with a filename that is the same as the last component of the non-directory file.
  PASS DIR FILE: the same rule with the directory first -- If only one of file1 and file2 is a directory...
  PASS FILE DIR with identical contents: exit 0 -- If only one of file1 and file2 is a directory...
  PASS FILE DIR with no DIR/basename: exit 2 -- >1 An error occurred.
  PASS the same FILE twice is identical (no second open) -- 0 No differences were found. (ProDOS refuses a second OPEN)
  PASS -r on the same directory twice is identical -- 0 No differences were found.
  PASS -r with a missing operand: exit 2 -- >1 An error occurred.
  PASS -r orders mixed-case names by BYTE, not as ls does -- the collating sequence of the current locale -- C: bytes
  PASS -r with a trailing slash prints the same header -- diff -r A/F B/F -- the slash is not doubled
  PASS -r at 100 entries per directory, staged out of order -- Only in ... and diff lines, all in collating order
  PASS -r at the depth bound: 16 nested levels still compare -- a recursive diff has no depth limit; maux bounds it at 16
  PASS -r one level past the bound is refused loudly -- maux bound: DMAXDEP levels; deeper is exit 2, never a silently shortened walk
       hand-authored: DMAXDEP is 16 saved levels (diffrec.s). The hosts have no such bound; this is a stated maux limit, so the expectation is authored here: nothing on stdout, exit 2, and the differing file at the bottom is NOT reached (a partial walk would be a wrong answer).
  PASS -r whose listings do not fit the name arena: exit 2 -- maux bound: NAMESZ; an unlisted directory is never compared
       hand-authored: 360 + 360 entries at 17 bytes is 12,240 > NAMESZ 12,000 (diff.s). The trees are IDENTICAL, so a truncated comparison would have said exit 0; the loud failure is exit 2 with nothing on stdout.
  PASS directory vs file of the same name is reported (rc 1) -- File %s is a directory while file %s is a regular file
       hand-authored: Both hosts print this line; GNU exits 1 and BSD exits 0. POSIX makes any difference 1.
  PASS file vs directory of the same name is reported (rc 1) -- File %s is a regular file while file %s is a directory
       hand-authored: Same hosts divergence as the row above (GNU 1, BSD 0); the order of the two kinds follows the operands.
  PASS FILE DIR whose DIR/basename is a directory is reported -- File %s is a regular file while file %s is a directory
       hand-authored: GNU 1, BSD 0 on a stat mismatch; POSIX 1.
  PASS -r with "." as an operand keeps the typed spelling -- diff -r ./SUB/X D2/SUB/X -- "." resolves, the display stays
       hand-authored: The case's own base holds D2 and OUT (the shell created OUT for the redirect before DIFF ran) beside SUB, so a host run on a scratch cwd cannot see the same tree; the expectation is authored. D2 and OUT sort before SUB, so their lines come first.
  PASS round trip: patch applies what diff -u wrote -- The [diff] output... shall be sufficient to convert file1 into file2 [and] patch shall... apply those differences
       hand-authored: the assertion is a filesystem side effect -- maux's diff writes a unified patch to OUT, then a `post` step runs maux's PATCH over it in the same shell session, and F1 must come back byte-identical to F2. oracle()'s host path runs exactly ONE process per case and never a post step (check_posixutil._host_once has no such hook), so a post-verified result is necessarily hand-authored, the same shape as mkdir's '-p creates missing intermediate components'. The expected stdout is maux's own -u output for this fixture, which the '-u unified context output' row above compares against the host byte for byte (the header lines carry no timestamp -- see check_posixutil.NORMALIZERS).

== dirname (5 cases) ==
  PASS no slash at all: "." -- if string does not contain a slash ... dirname shall write the string "." 
  PASS trailing slashes stripped before the last component is removed -- if string consists of ... trailing slashes, [remove them] before proceeding
  PASS plain: everything but the last component -- the pathname of the parent directory of that file. Trailing <slash> characters ... shall be removed
  PASS nested: strips exactly one component -- the string shall be modified by removing ... trailing <slash> characters and ... the last component
  PASS a string of nothing but slashes is "/" -- if string is composed entirely of <slash> characters ... dirname shall write '/' 

== du (4 cases) ==
  PASS -s summary: one grand total per argument -- Instead of the default output, report only the total sum.
       hand-authored: ProDOS block accounting, not a byte count a host filesystem shares: each small staged file is a seedling (1 data block, <512B), and the directory D itself costs its own key block (du.s header: a subdirectory's total 'SEEDS at its own blocks_used') -- 1(D) + 1(A) + 1(B) = 3. No host `du` reports ProDOS block units, so this is hand, not host-oracled.
  PASS -a also lists every plain file -- In addition to the default output, report the size of each file not of type directory.
       hand-authored: same block accounting as du -s's own case; order is du.s's documented deepest-first post-order walk (files before their enclosing directory's own total), file order within D is ProDOS directory-entry (creation) order, matching this harness's own staging order.
  PASS -k kilobyte units -- Write the files sizes in units of 1024 bytes, rather than the default 512-byte units.
       hand-authored: SHIPPED 2026-09-17: TOKB converts U2DEC_VAL to (blocks+1)/2 right before PRLINE prints it (ceiling -- an odd 512-byte block is still a whole KB once rounded up), leaving every accumulator counting real ProDOS blocks throughout the walk. D + A is 2 512-byte blocks (D's own key block, A's one seedling data block, same accounting as the -s case above) -> 1 KB unit. No host `du` reports ProDOS block units, so this is hand, not host-oracled, like every other du row here.
  PASS a trailing slash on an operand is not doubled -- du [-a|-s] [-kx] [file...] -- the path of each entry is the operand joined to the entry name.
       hand-authored: docs/DU-DF.md: du joined PATH + '/' + name unconditionally, so `du D/` printed D//A (BSD du still does; GNU du prints D/A). One '/' between the parts, and the total line echoes the operand as typed, D/. Same block accounting as the -a row above. No host `du` reports ProDOS block units, so this is hand, not host-oracled.

== echo (3 cases) ==
  PASS arguments joined by single spaces, CR-terminated -- A string to be written to standard output.
  PASS no arguments prints just a newline -- write to standard output... followed by a <newline>.
  PASS -n as argv[1] suppresses the trailing newline -- the results are implementation-defined (this implementation: argv[1] exactly '-n' suppresses the trailing newline, matched against a live /bin/echo -- see echo.s's own header)

== env (8 cases) ==
  PASS -i with no operands: an empty environment, printed -- -i ... Invoke utility (or write to standard output the resulting environment) with exactly the environment specified by the arguments
  PASS a name=value operand, no utility: printed -- arguments of the form name=value ... shall be added to the environment
  PASS a utility is resolved and spawned -- the environment for utility shall be the current environment ... utility shall be invoked
  PASS a name=value operand is merged before the spawn -- options and any arguments to those options ... [then] arguments of the form name=value ... modify the environment
  PASS the spawned utility SEES the merged environment -- the environment for utility shall be the current environment ... modified by the name=value arguments
  PASS a duplicate name=value: only the LAST survives -- arguments of the form name=value shall be added to the environment ... [each] shall replace any existing variable of that name
  PASS exit status is the utility's own -- Otherwise, the exit status of env shall be the exit status of utility
       hand-authored: false's own exit status (1) passed through unchanged -- checked live against this host's own /usr/bin/env, which agrees.
  PASS utility not found on PATH: exit 127 -- 127 if utility could not be invoked. Some other value if utility was invoked but failed
       hand-authored: diagnostic wording is not compared here (check_posixutil never captures stderr, same posture as PRINTF_CASES's own numeric-error row); only the exit status (127) is checked, matched to this host's own /usr/bin/env, which agrees.

== expand (4 cases) ==
  PASS tabs are replaced with the spaces needed to reach the next tab stop; default stops every 8 columns -- expand ... writes ... with tabs changed to spaces ... default tab stops shall be at each column position n such that n modulo 8 is equal to 1.
  PASS -t N sets stops every N columns, without limit -- -t tablist ... If a single number is given ... tab stops shall be set that number of column positions apart, instead of the default 8.
  PASS -t list sets explicit stops; past the last one a tab becomes a single space -- -t tablist ... a list ... shall set the tab stops at those column positions.
  PASS a backspace decrements the column and is written to the output as itself -- expand ... [a <backspace>] shall decrement the column count by 1 ... and shall be copied to the output.

== expr (64 cases) ==
  PASS addition -- expr1 + expr2 -- Addition of decimal integer-valued arguments.
  PASS subtraction to zero is falsy -- The expression evaluated to null or zero... exit 1.
  PASS multiplication -- expr1 * expr2 -- Multiplication of decimal integer-valued arguments.
  PASS integer division -- expr1 / expr2 -- Integer division of decimal integer-valued arguments, producing an integer result.
  PASS remainder -- expr1 % expr2 -- Remainder of integer division of decimal integer-valued arguments.
  PASS numeric equal -- expr1 = expr2 -- Equal.
  PASS numeric not-equal -- expr1 != expr2 -- Not equal.
  PASS less-than -- expr1 < expr2 -- Less than.
  PASS less-or-equal -- expr1 <= expr2 -- Less than or equal.
  PASS greater-than -- expr1 > expr2 -- Greater than.
  PASS greater-or-equal -- expr1 >= expr2 -- Greater than or equal.
  PASS AND both truthy -- expr1 & expr2 -- Returns the evaluation of expr1 if neither expression evaluates to null or zero; otherwise, returns zero.
  PASS AND left falsy yields zero -- expr1 & expr2 -- ...otherwise, returns zero.
  PASS OR left truthy -- expr1 | expr2 -- Returns the evaluation of expr1 if it is neither null nor zero...
  PASS OR left falsy falls through -- expr1 | expr2 -- ...otherwise, returns the evaluation of expr2 if it is not null...
  PASS string equality true -- Returns the result of a decimal integer comparison if both arguments are integers; otherwise, returns the result of a string comparison...
  PASS string equality false (non-numeric operands) -- otherwise, returns the result of a string comparison using the locale-specific collation sequence.
  PASS precedence: * binds tighter than + -- The expressions are listed in order of decreasing precedence ... All of the operators shall be left-associative.
  PASS leftover token is a syntax error -- 2 Invalid expression error.
  PASS division by zero: 16-bit maux vs. 64-bit BSD -- >2 Another error occurred.
       hand-authored: POSIX permits ANY code >2 for 'another error occurred' and 2 for 'invalid expression'; BSD expr (this Mac's oracle) picks 2 for div-by-zero ('expr: division by zero', verified live), while maux's own EX_DODIV/EX_DOMOD (src/bin/expr.ops.s) documents and sets EX_STATUS=3 specifically for a zero divisor. Both are POSIX-legal; they are not the SAME code, so BSD cannot oracle this row.
  PASS overflow: 16-bit maux vs. 64-bit BSD -- 2 Invalid expression error.
       hand-authored: maux's numbers are unsigned 16-bit only (expr.s header: 'NUMBERS: unsigned 16-bit only (0-65535)... OVERFLOW CHOICE: LOUD ERROR'), so 60000+60000 (120000) overflows and EXPR_EXITCD/EX_NPARSE force status 2. A 64-bit BSD host computes 120000 successfully and exits 0 -- not a shared construct, so BSD cannot oracle it; the expectation is maux's own documented contract, not a captured run.
  PASS ':' matching operator: length of an anchored match (retired known-gap row) -- expr1 : expr2 -- Matching expression... compare the string resulting from the evaluation of expr1 with the regular expression pattern resulting from the evaluation of expr2.
  PASS ':' a pattern that matches only PART of the string returns that length -- expr1 : expr2 ... the pattern shall be anchored to the beginning of the string
  PASS ':' is anchored: a match further along is no match (result 0, exit 1) -- expr1 : expr2 ... implicitly anchored at the beginning
  PASS ':' with .* is the string's length -- expr1 : expr2 ... number of characters matched
  PASS ':' of the empty string matches .* with length 0 -- expr1 : expr2 ... number of characters matched; 0 on failure
  PASS ':' with a group returns the group's text -- ...if the pattern contains at least one BRE subexpression \(...\), the string corresponding to \1 shall be returned
  PASS ':' returns the FIRST group of several -- ...the string corresponding to \1 shall be returned
  PASS ':' group inside a longer pattern returns the group (the tail) -- ...the string corresponding to \1 shall be returned
  PASS ':' group that matched empty returns the empty string, exit 1 -- ...the string corresponding to \1 shall be returned
  PASS ':' group in a pattern that does not match: empty string, exit 1 -- ...or a null string if the pattern does not match
  PASS ':' backreference inside the pattern -- BRE: \(...\) and \1 are in the basic regular expression grammar
  PASS ':' backreference that fails: empty, exit 1 -- BRE: \(...\) and \1 are in the basic regular expression grammar
  PASS ':' a leading ^ is the (redundant) anchor -- expr1 : expr2 ... implicitly anchored
  PASS ':' a SECOND ^ is a literal one -- expr1 : expr2 ... implicitly anchored; a ^ not at the start of a BRE is an ordinary character
  PASS ':' a trailing $ anchors the end -- BRE: $ anchors at the end of the string
  PASS ':' a trailing $ that does not reach the end fails -- BRE: $ anchors at the end of the string
  PASS ':' an interval -- BRE: \{m,n\} intervals
  PASS ':' a bracket expression with a class and a group -- BRE: bracket expressions with character classes
  PASS ':' a leading ] in a bracket is a member -- BRE: a ] first in a bracket expression is a member
  PASS ':' an escaped dot is a literal dot -- BRE: \. is a literal period
  PASS ':' a numeric operand matches its text as TYPED (leading zeros kept) -- expr1 : expr2 ... the string resulting from the evaluation of expr1
  PASS ':' numeric operands on both sides -- expr1 : expr2 ... the string resulting from the evaluation of expr1
  PASS ':' group over a zero-padded number, then the tail -- ...the string corresponding to \1 shall be returned
  PASS ':' binds TIGHTER than * (the match is the left operand of the multiplication) -- The expressions are listed in order of decreasing precedence: expr1 : expr2 ... expr1 * expr2
  PASS ':' binds tighter than * on the RIGHT as well -- The expressions are listed in order of decreasing precedence: expr1 : expr2 ... expr1 * expr2
  PASS ':' binds tighter than + -- The expressions are listed in order of decreasing precedence: expr1 : expr2 ... expr1 + expr2
  PASS ':' is left associative -- The expressions are listed in order of decreasing precedence
  PASS ':' binds tighter than = (its result compares) -- The expressions are listed in order of decreasing precedence: expr1 : expr2 ... expr1 = expr2
  PASS ':' a parenthesised computed number is the text -- ( expr ) -- Grouping symbols
  PASS ':' a long group prints WHOLE (no result buffer to truncate) -- ...the string corresponding to \1 shall be returned
  PASS ':' a literal ( inside a bracket is not a group (result is the length, not text) -- BRE: inside a bracket expression a backslash is an ordinary character
  PASS ':' an escaped backslash before ( is not a group -- BRE: \\ is a literal backslash
  PASS ':' a leading * is a literal star -- BRE: a * first in an expression is an ordinary character
  PASS ':' a '-1' operand is text, not an option -- expr1 : expr2
  PASS ':' group text that is a decimal integer takes part in arithmetic -- expr1 + expr2 -- Addition of decimal integer-valued arguments
       hand-authored: measured: the box's GNU expr prints 11; this Mac's BSD expr REFUSES it with 'not a decimal number: 10' (exit 2), treating the matched text as opaque. The XCU text makes the result of ':' an ordinary expression value, and a string of decimal digits is an integer there. maux follows GNU/XCU: a canonical decimal group (no leading zero) is a number.
  PASS ':' a group of all zeros is zero: exit 1, text kept -- Exit status: 1 -- The expression evaluated to null or zero
       hand-authored: measured: both hosts print 00; GNU exits 1 (zero), BSD exits 0 (it calls any non-empty string true except a lone 0). XCU: 'null or zero' -> 1. maux follows GNU/XCU, and prints the text as the pattern matched it, not as a number (EX_FALSCK's all-zeros rule).
  PASS ':' a group of a lone 0 is zero: exit 1 -- Exit status: 1 -- The expression evaluated to null or zero
       hand-authored: measured: GNU exit 1, BSD exit 0 (BSD treats the matched string '0' as true). XCU: zero -> exit 1.
  PASS ':' a leading * in the pattern is an ordinary character: no match -- BRE: a * first in an expression is an ordinary character
       hand-authored: measured: GNU prints 0 and exits 1; BSD refuses with 'syntax error' (exit 2). POSIX BRE reads a leading * as a literal, which matches nothing in 'foo'.
  PASS ':' an empty pattern matches the empty prefix: 0, exit 1 -- expr1 : expr2 (the XCU text is silent on an empty BRE)
       hand-authored: measured: GNU prints 0 and exits 1; BSD refuses with 'empty (sub)expression' (exit 2). An empty BRE matches an empty string at the start, whose length is 0.
  PASS ':' group text times a number -- expr1 * expr2 -- Multiplication of decimal integer-valued arguments
       hand-authored: measured: GNU 24; BSD refuses (exit 2) for the same reason as the + row above.
  GAP  ':' a malformed BRE is a usage error (exit 2) on the hosts -- expr1 : expr2 ... > 1 An error occurred.
       known gap: regex.s has no compile step (the Fowler/Spencer report counts 28 regcomp-error rows 'reported but unscored' for the same reason): a malformed BRE is a SAFE NO-MATCH, so maux prints an empty line and exits 1 where BSD ('parentheses not balanced') and GNU ('Unmatched ( or \(') both exit 2. The same holds for an unterminated [. Giving the engine a validating pass is a change to the shared library, not to expr.
  PASS parentheses group -- ( expr ) -- Grouping symbols. Any expression can be placed within parentheses.
  PASS parentheses override precedence -- ( expr ) -- Grouping symbols. Any expression can be placed within parentheses.

== false (2 cases) ==
  PASS returns a non-zero exit code -- The false utility shall return with a non-zero exit code.
  PASS ignores operands -- OPERANDS -- None.

== find (42 cases) ==
  PASS -name matches the basename against a glob -- true if the basename of the current pathname matches pattern
  PASS -type d matches directories only -- true if the type of the file is c... 'd'
  PASS -type f matches plain files only -- true if the type of the file is c... 'f'
  PASS -exec spawns per match with {} substituted -- The utility named by the utility... shall be invoked... {} shall be replaced by the current pathname
       hand-authored: the exec'd command word is 'ECHO', maux's own binary name (findact.s's BUILD_EXECV/PATHRSLV resolves it via PATH exactly as execvp would) -- the host has no such binary (only lowercase 'echo'), and argv is shared verbatim between the maux and host renders, so a host run of this exact case would fail on a missing command rather than test -exec's own behaviour. Hand-authored to findact.s's own ACT_EXEC/BUILD_EXECV: {} substitutes the SAME walked-path text a plain -print would write, here 'TREE/A.TXT', and -exec's child inherits find's own stdout unredirected.
  PASS -newer: no fixture file is ever strictly newer -- true if the modification time of the current file is more recent than the modification time of the file named by pathname
       hand-authored: findexpr.s's TESTNEWER/DTGT do a real GET_FILE_INFO compare now (no longer a USAGE_ERR) -- but every file this harness stages lands in the SAME a2til write() call, which timestamps with datetime.now().replace(second=0, microsecond=0): ProDOS's own mod_time word is minute-granularity, so every fixture file in one build shares ONE mod_date/mod_time (barring the sub-second chance a build straddles a minute boundary). '-newer' is strict ('more recent than'), so a tie is FALSE for every entry, including TREE and TREE/B.BIN against the reference TREE/A.TXT -- proving the real comparison RUNS (exit 0, not 2) without needing a controlled clock to prove the TRUE branch too.
  PASS -prune stops descent but still prints the pruned dir -- cause find not to descend the current pathname if it is a directory
       hand-authored: findexpr.s's FACT_PRIM/TT_PRUNE sets PRUNEFLAG (findtop.s's WALK checks it right after EVAL_TOP and skips the descend even though SUB is a directory) -- INNER.TXT is never visited, proving non-descent, not just the print. TREE itself and A.TXT do not match '-name SUB' so -prune never runs for them (find is still descended into TREE itself, since prune's own primary was never reached there).
  PASS default -print applies to the WHOLE -o expression -- if the given expression does not contain any of the primaries -exec, -ok, or -print, the given expression shall be effectively replaced by ( given expression) -print
       hand-authored: no -exec/-print anywhere in the expression, so EVAL_TOP (findexpr.s) prints only when the WHOLE '-name A.TXT -o -name B.BIN' came back true, regardless of which side of the -o decided it -- proving the default-print rewrite is not merely 'always print', it is gated on the real boolean result.
  PASS -a binds tighter than -o (no explicit parens) -- expr -a expr ... in order of decreasing precedence
       hand-authored: parses as '-name A.TXT -o (-name B.BIN -a -type d)'. TREE/A.TXT: left side true, prints regardless of the right. TREE/B.BIN: left false, right is '-name B.BIN -a -type d' -- B.BIN IS a plain file, so -type d is false and the AND is false -- NOT printed. If -a bound LOOSER than -o (wrong precedence: '(-name A.TXT -o -name B.BIN) -a -type d'), TREE/A.TXT would ALSO fail -type d and nothing would print at all -- this case's non-empty, single-line answer is exactly the wrong-precedence tell.
  PASS explicit ( expr -o expr ) grouping parses -- ( expression ) True if expression is true
       hand-authored: EVAL_FACT's TT_LPAR branch actually consumes '(' ... ')' and recurses into EVAL_EXPR for the grouped '-o' -- proving the parenthesis tokens parse and evaluate, not merely that this particular pair of files would print the same without them.
  PASS ! negates a primary -- ! -- Negation of a primary; the unary NOT operator
       hand-authored: findexpr.s's EVAL_FACT TT_NOT branch EORs the sub-factor's result. TREE and TREE/SUB (both directories, basenames 'TREE'/'SUB') do not match '*.TXT' so '!' makes them true and default-print fires; TREE/A.TXT DOES match '*.TXT' so '!' makes it false.
  PASS -exec inside an -o arm only runs when reached -- -o ... The second expression shall not be evaluated if the first expression is true
       hand-authored: '-name NOPE.TXT' matches nothing in this tree, so the -o's LEFT side is false for EVERY visited node and the RIGHT side (-exec ECHO {} ;) runs for every one of them -- TREE (root) first, then SUB, A.TXT, B.BIN in on-disk order -- proving an action nested inside an -o arm is reachable and fires exactly when the grammar says it should (ANYACTION=1 from the -exec token, so no default -print besides). Same 'ECHO is maux's own binary' reasoning as the plain -exec case above.
  PASS -size N matches exact 512-byte-block count -- the file size in bytes, divided by 512 and rounded up... is n
       hand-authored: TESTSIZE (findtst.s) OPENs+GET_EOFs each file fresh. A.TXT is 2 bytes on disk ('x'+CR) -> ceil(2/512)=1 block, matches '-size 1'. B.BIN is 40 lines of 16 bytes ('0123456789ABCDE'+CR) = 640 bytes -> ceil(640/512)=2 blocks, does not match.
  PASS -size +N and the c (bytes) suffix -- If n is followed by the character 'c', the size shall be in bytes
       hand-authored: A.TXT is exactly 2 bytes on disk ('x' + one CR) -- '-size 2c' is an exact byte match, exercising TESTSIZE's TT_SIZEC path (no rounding) rather than the default 512-byte-block path the case above covers.
  PASS -links: every maux file has exactly one link -- true if the file has n links
       hand-authored: TESTLINKS (findtst.s) never stats anything -- ProDOS/maux has no hard-link support at all, so the 'measured' link count is the constant 1 for every file, compared via the same +/-/exact CMPSIGN every numeric primary uses. '-links 2' (not staged as its own case) would match nothing, for the same reason.
  PASS -path matches the WHOLE walked pathname, not the basename -- true if the current pathname matches pattern
       hand-authored: '*/A.TXT' requires a '/' before the name -- '-name' could never match this pattern (it globs the bare basename only, never a '/'); TESTPATH (findtst.s) globs EVPATH (the full walked path) instead of EVBASE, proving the two primaries read different subjects.
  PASS -xdev is accepted and changes nothing (single device) -- cause find not to descend below directories that have a different device ID
       hand-authored: ProDOS has no multiple-device-per-tree concept find could ever cross within one walk, so '-xdev' is accepted as a constant-true no-op (TT_XDEV in FACT_PRIM) -- ANDed with '-name A.TXT' it must not change the result versus that primary alone.
  PASS -nouser/-nogroup: no user database exists at all -- true if the file belongs to a user ID for which getpwuid returns NULL
       hand-authored: ProDOS carries no owner/group concept at all, so EVERY file's (nonexistent) owner has no corresponding name -- '-nouser'/'-nogroup' are honestly constant-true (TT_NOUSER/TT_NOGROUP), not stubs; ANDed with '-name A.TXT' the result must be unchanged from that primary alone.
  PASS -mtime is false when the clock is absent -- the file modification time subtracted from the initialization time, divided by 86400... is n
       hand-authored: reset={'clock': False} pins the Timemaster card off, the same determinism trick date.s's own NOCLOCK-path cases use (see this module's header). GETNOW's validity check (findtst.s, same rule as date.s/ls.s) then leaves CLOCKOK=0, and TESTMTIME makes -mtime FALSE for every file -- so '-name A.TXT -a -mtime 0' prints NOTHING even though the name half matches, per this task's own instruction ('if the clock is absent make it false'). A live-clock day-difference value is NOT exercised here (no synchronized-peek mechanism for a bare case list, the same gap check_sumdate.py exists to close for `date`/`sum` -- see TESTMTIME's own header for the calendar-day-vs-86400-seconds approximation it would measure if it were).
  GAP  -perm has no ProDOS meaning (held red, permanent) -- -perm [-]mode: true if the file permission bits match mode
       known gap: PERMANENT. ProDOS keeps a destroy/rename/backup/write/read access byte, not rwx for a user, a group and the world: there is no honest octal mapping to match a mode against (docs/ANACHRONISMS.md sec W2 rules a Unix rwx mapping out). -perm is not a row in findexpr.s's flag table, so TOKENIZE falls through to TOKBADFLG, exit 2; BSD and GNU find accept it and exit 0.
  GAP  -user has no ProDOS meaning (held red, permanent) -- -user uname: true if the file belongs to the user uname
       known gap: PERMANENT. ProDOS files have no owner and this machine has no user database, so there is no name to resolve and nothing to compare it with. -user falls through TOKENIZE to TOKBADFLG, exit 2; BSD and GNU find accept it.
  GAP  -group has no ProDOS meaning (held red, permanent) -- -group gname: true if the file belongs to the group gname
       known gap: PERMANENT. As -user: ProDOS files have no group and this machine has no group database. -group falls through TOKENIZE to TOKBADFLG, exit 2; the hosts accept it (BSD exit 0, GNU exit 1 when the group does not exist -- neither is 2).
  PASS a tree walked without -depth is listed parent first -- The pathname ... of the starting point shall be written before the entries it contains (the default order).
  PASS -depth lists a directory after its contents -- -depth -- Always true; causes descent of the directory hierarchy to be done so that all entries in a directory are acted on before the directory itself.
  PASS -depth -print is the same order as -depth alone -- -depth -- ... all entries in a directory are acted on before the directory itself.
  PASS -depth -type d lists the directories deepest first -- -depth -- ... acted on before the directory itself.
  PASS -depth after the other primaries is still global -- -depth -- Always true ... it applies to the whole walk wherever it appears in the expression.
  PASS -depth -name filters a post-order walk -- -name pattern ... -depth ...
  PASS -depth ! negates inside a post-order walk -- ! expression ... -depth ...
  PASS -depth makes -prune a no-op -- -prune -- ... If the -depth primary is specified, the -prune primary shall have no effect.
  PASS -prune without -depth still prunes (control) -- -prune -- ... shall cause find not to descend the current pathname if it is a directory.
  PASS -depth on a plain file operand lists just the file -- -depth -- ... applied to a file operand.
  PASS -depth on a directory operand with one file -- -depth -- ... acted on before the directory itself.
  PASS -depth with -exec runs the command in post-order -- -exec ... -depth ...
  PASS -ok a y runs the utility -- -ok utility_name [argument ...] ; ... the user is asked whether to execute it; a "y" causes the execution.
  PASS -ok an n runs nothing and prints nothing -- -ok ... an affirmative response is required to execute.
  PASS -ok Y and a word that begins with y are affirmative -- -ok ... an affirmative response ...
  PASS -ok an empty reply declines -- -ok ... a reply that is not affirmative does not execute.
  PASS -ok suppresses the implicit -print -- If the expression contains none of -exec, -ok, and -print, the given expression shall be effectively replaced by ( given_expression ) -print.
  PASS -ok then -print: a y lets the -print through -- -ok ... is true if the utility exits 0.
  PASS -ok then -print: an n makes -ok false, so no -print -- -ok ... is false if the user declines.
  PASS -ok of a failing utility is false (-o takes the arm) -- -ok ... true if the utility exits with a zero status.
  PASS -depth -ok asks in post-order, one answer per path -- -ok ... -depth ...

== fold (4 cases) ==
  PASS breaks lines at a default width of 80 columns -- fold ... breaking the lines ... The default ... shall be 80.
  PASS -w width sets an explicit width -- -w width -- Specify the maximum line length, in column positions ..., rather than the default of 80.
  PASS -b counts bytes rather than column positions -- -b -- Count width in bytes rather than column positions.
  PASS -s breaks after the last blank within the width -- -s -- ... break the line after the last <blank> character that is within the first width column positions.

== grep (16 cases) ==
  PASS basic BRE match -- an input line shall be selected if any pattern, treated as an entire basic regular expression (BRE)... matches any part of the line
  PASS -c counts selected lines -- -c Write only a count of selected lines to standard output.
  PASS -i case-insensitive -- -i Perform pattern matching in a case-insensitive manner...
  PASS -l names files, once, on multiple operands -- -l ... Pathnames shall be written once per file searched.
  PASS -n precedes each line by its number -- -n Precede each output line by its relative line number in the file, each file starting at line 1.
  PASS -q exits zero on a later match despite an earlier open error -- If the -q option is specified, the exit status shall be zero if an input line is selected, even if an error was detected.
  PASS -s suppresses the open-error message -- -s Suppress the error messages ordinarily written for nonexistent or unreadable files.
  PASS -v inverts the match -- -v Select lines not matching any of the specified patterns.
  PASS -x matches the whole line only -- -x Consider only input lines that use all characters in the line... to match an entire fixed string or regular expression
  PASS -e supplies the pattern as an option-argument -- -e pattern_list -- Specify one or more patterns to be used during the search for input.
  PASS multiple FILE operands get a FILE: prefix -- "%s:", <file> -- ...more than one file argument appears, and -q is not specified...
  PASS unopenable file: exit>1 -- >1 An error occurred.
  GAP  -E extended regular expressions is unimplemented -- -E Match using extended regular expressions.
       known gap: grep.s's GETOPT table (GREPOPTS/the option switch in START) has no 'E' case at all -- 'E' falls through to :usage, exit 2. BSD grep -E treats '+' as one-or-more and matches, exit 0.
  PASS -F matches a fixed string, not a BRE -- -F Match using fixed strings.
  PASS -f reads patterns from a file -- -f pattern_file -- Read one or more patterns from the file named by the pathname pattern_file.
  PASS a second -e accumulates: either pattern selects -- -e pattern_list -- Specify one or more patterns... A line shall be selected if it matches any of the specified patterns.

== head (8 cases) ==
  PASS default is -n 10 -- If no options are specified, head shall act as if -n 10 had been specified.
  PASS -n N explicit count -- -n number -- ...the ending location in the file shall be measured in lines...
  PASS -N obsolescent form -- Earlier versions of this standard allowed a -number option. This form is no longer specified by POSIX.1-2024 but may be present in some implementations.
  PASS short file copied in its entirety -- When a file contains less than number bytes or lines, it shall be copied to standard output in its entirety. This shall not be an error.
  PASS multiple files get ==> NAME <== headers -- "\n==> %s <==\n", <pathname> -- except that the first header written shall not include the initial <newline>.
  PASS no operands reads stdin -- The standard input shall be used if no file operands are specified...
  PASS missing file continues, exit>0 -- >0 An error occurred.
  PASS -c takes a byte count -- -c number -- The first number bytes of each input file shall be copied to standard output.

== join (10 cases) ==
  PASS the plain join: field 1 by default, blanks the separator -- join ... the join field ... the DEFAULT ... field one
  PASS -t sets a single-character field separator, both sides -- -t char ... Use character ... as ... separator
  PASS -1/-2 select a different join field per file -- -1 field_number ... -2 field_number
  PASS -a 1 adds file1's unpaired lines to the output -- -a file_number ... add ... unpairable line
  PASS -a 2 adds file2's unpaired lines to the output -- -a file_number ... add ... unpairable line
  PASS -v 1 prints ONLY file1's unpaired lines -- -v file_number ... produce a line for each unpairable line
  PASS -o selects and orders output fields, 0 is the join field -- -o list ... 0 ... the join field
  PASS -e replaces an EMPTY -o field (the XCU/GNU rule; BSD differs) -- -e string ... replace ... empty ... output field
       hand-authored: GNU and the XCU both apply -e ONLY through -o (join.s's own header); BSD's own -e differs (unconditional substitution outside -o too), so this row is pinned to the XCU wording rather than host-oracled against this Mac's BSD join. Verified live against this host's own /usr/bin/join for the SHAPE (field 2.2 of the unpaired '4 delta' record prints 'X') before pinning the exact text.
  PASS a line with no partner is simply absent (no -a) -- join ... without -a ... only paired lines
  PASS "-" names stdin for one operand -- file1 ... file2 ... A pathname of "-" ... standard input

== kill (5 cases) ==
  PASS -0 on the interactive shell: a live, ordinary pid -- the symbolic name 0 shall be recognized, representing the signal value zero
       hand-authored: $$ names the INTERACTIVE SHELL kill was spawned from. Measured live (this module's own first gate run): exit 0, not the root-tier-protected 'cannot kill' this case first assumed -- $$ is an ORDINARY process (see this file's own KILL_CASES header comment on the -9 finding that corrected this). PRC_KILL (src/kernel/procspawn2.s) reads $$'s PCB, finds a nonzero, non-root parent, and takes the signo==0 probe branch: ':probed', OK, no kill. No host process shares this pid space, so this stays hand-authored -- to a value confirmed by running it, not assumed.
  PASS -0 on an out-of-range pid: no such process -- if pid is not an existing process, kill shall fail and no signal shall be sent
       hand-authored: PCB_COUNT is 48 (inc/mm.annex.equ) -- pid 200 fails PRC_KILL's own range check before any PCB read, ERR_SRCH ('no such process'). rc 1 is what POSIX asks for ('>0 An error occurred') and what the host's /bin/kill answers for a missing pid (`kill -0 99999` -> rc 1, 'No such process'; measured on Darwin 25.5). THIS ROW PINNED A DEFECT UNTIL KFIX 4: kill.s counted only SUCCESSFUL pids toward 'had operands', so a run in which every pid failed fell through to USAGE and exited 2 with a usage line after the diagnostic. It was a hand= row recording what the binary did, not an oracle; it now records the correct status. Still hand-authored: no host process shares this pid space, and the diagnostic goes to stderr.
  PASS no operands is a usage error -- >0 An error occurred.
       hand-authored: kill.s's own :done: NPIDS==0 (no WELL-FORMED pid operand: none was given, or every one was malformed; a well-formed pid that then FAILS counts since KFIX 4) is a usage error, not a silent no-op -- 'kill -9' alone hits the identical path (kill.s's own comment: 'a usage error, not a nop'). exit 2 is this tree's uniform usage-error convention (comm.s/cmp.s/etc.), which POSIX's generic '>0' permits.
  PASS -l lists signal names -- Write all values of signal_name supported by the implementation, if no operand is given.
       hand-authored: SHIPPED 2026-09-17: LISTSIGS (kill.s) prints the same 5-name table MATCHSIG's -s now accepts, signal-number order (HUP=1 INT=2 QUIT=3 KILL=9 TERM=15), space-separated, one line, exit 0. Real BSD kill -l lists 30+ signals this kernel does not have, so this stays hand-authored to maux's own reduced set, not host-oracled.
  PASS -s signal_name (by name) -- Specify the signal to send, using one of the symbolic names defined in the tables of the signal.h page.
       hand-authored: SHIPPED 2026-09-17: -s NAME runs through MATCHSIG (kill.s), the same 5-name table timeout.s's own -s uses (HUP/INT/QUIT/KILL/TERM). Pid 200 fails PRC_KILL's range check (ERR_SRCH) whichever real signal -s resolved to, so the status is the failed-pid status, 1 -- proving -s's NAME lookup reached SC_KILL at all, not that pid 200 exists. Pinned rc=2 until KFIX 4 (the every-pid-failed run fell through to USAGE; see the '-0 on an out-of-range pid' row). No host process shares this pid space, so this stays hand-authored.

== ls (8 cases) ==
  PASS redirected stdout is one name per line (not a console) -- The default format shall be to list one entry per line to standard output; the exceptions are to terminals or when one of the -C, -m, or -x options is specified.
  PASS -1 forces one name per line explicitly -- -1 Force output to be one entry per line.
  PASS -r reverses the (name) sort order -- Reverse the order of the sort to get reverse collating sequence
  PASS -C columns even though stdout is not a console -- Write multi-text-column output with entries sorted down the columns
       hand-authored: lscol.s's COLSETUP: not a console + -C given -> width 80 (its own header: '80 for -C on a file or pipe'); LSCOLW = longest name (1) + 2 = 3; NCOLS = 80/3 = 26; with only 3 entries, NROWS = ceil(3/26) = 1, so all three land in row 0 (column-major with one row degenerates to left-to-right) padded to LSCOLW except the last -- structural, no host equivalent for THIS width policy (BSD ls -C sizes columns to $COLUMNS or 80 too, but pads differently), hand-keyed to lscol.s's own PRINTCOLS rather than captured.
  PASS -d list the directory itself, not its contents -- Do not follow symbolic links... Do not treat directories differently than other types of files.
  PASS -R recursively lists subdirectories -- Recursively list subdirectories encountered.
  PASS -F appends a slash to a directory -- Write a <slash> ('/') immediately after each pathname that is a directory
  PASS -s reports ProDOS blocks used -- -s Indicate the total number of file system blocks consumed by each file displayed.
       hand-authored: a2til stages this one-byte seedling file in one ProDOS 512-byte block; maux reports the directory entry blocks_used field directly.

== make (40 cases) ==
  PASS macro expands in a command line -- Macros ... $(string1) ... shall be replaced by string2.
  PASS brace form ${name} is the same macro -- The macro shall be referenced as $(string1) or ${string1}.
  PASS single-character macro needs no parentheses -- If string1 is a single character, the parentheses are optional.
  PASS $$ is a literal dollar sign -- The macro $$ shall be replaced by the single character $.
  PASS an undefined macro expands to nothing -- Macros that are never defined shall evaluate to a null string.
  PASS a macro value may reference another macro -- The macro shall be expanded when the command is used.
  PASS a macro expands in a dependency list -- Macros in the prerequisite list shall be evaluated.
  PASS a macro expands in a target name -- Macros shall be evaluated in target names.
  PASS a command-line macro overrides the makefile -- Macro definitions on the command line shall override definitions in the makefile.
  PASS $@ is the target being made -- The $@ macro shall evaluate to the full target name.
  PASS $< is the first prerequisite -- The $< macro shall evaluate to the prerequisite.
  PASS $? is every out-of-date prerequisite -- The $? macro shall evaluate to the list of prerequisites that are newer than the target.
  PASS a .s1.s2 rule builds a target with no rule of its own -- Inference rules ... The target ... .s1.s2 ... shall be used to build a file ending in s2 from one ending in s1.
  PASS $* is the stem of an inferred target -- The $* macro shall evaluate to the current target name with its suffix deleted.
  PASS inference serves many targets from one rule -- The same inference rule shall apply to every target matching its suffix.
  PASS a rule with prerequisites and no recipe infers one -- If the target has no commands, the inference rule shall supply them.
  PASS a suffix needs no leading dot -- The rule to create a file with suffix s2 that depends on a similarly named file with suffix s1 is specified as an entry for the target s1s2.
       hand-authored: GNU make cannot be the oracle: it requires a suffix to begin with a period, and this row is exactly the case where maux does not. make concatenates the pair to name the rule (`.S` + `R` = `.SR`) and never re-splits it, so the period is a convention rather than a requirement -- which is what lets the self-host MAKEFILE infer 512 objects named `CATMR` from sources named `CATM.S` without renaming a single generated file (docs/MAKE-POSIX.md sec 4).
  PASS an empty .SUFFIXES clears the list, so nothing infers -- Multiple lists accumulate; an empty list clears what came before.
       hand-authored: no shared oracle for the STATUS: POSIX pins only ">0" for a make error and GNU uses 2 where this make uses 1 throughout. The stdout both produce is empty.
  PASS the default target is the first NON-special rule -- If no target operand is given, the first target in the makefile shall be used; targets beginning with a period are not eligible.
  PASS one header, several targets, one recipe -- The first line of an entry is a blank-separated list of targets.
  PASS a command after a semicolon on the target line -- Text following a semicolon on the target line is a command.
  PASS a backslash continues a dependency line -- A line ending with a backslash shall be continued.
  PASS a backslash continues a command line -- A line ending with a backslash shall be continued.
       hand-authored: a DELIBERATE divergence, see this row's known_gap.
  PASS an @ command still prints under -n -- -n ... shall write the commands ... including lines beginning with an at-sign.
  PASS a whole-line # is a comment -- A comment starts with a number sign.
  PASS a # on a rule line is not a dependency -- A comment starts with a number sign.
  PASS -q exits 1 when the target is out of date -- -q ... Return a zero exit value if the target file is up to date; otherwise, return 1.
  PASS -f names the makefile and its name may be attached -- -f makefile  Specify a different makefile.
  PASS an unknown flag is a usage error, exit 2 -- The make utility shall exit with ... >0 An error occurred.
       hand-authored: no shared oracle for the STATUS: POSIX pins only ">0" for a make error and GNU uses 2 where this make uses 1 throughout. The stdout both produce is empty.
  PASS -s runs the recipe without echoing it -- -s  Do not write makefile command lines or touch messages to standard output before executing.
       hand-authored: runs a real recipe: the command differs by system, so there is no shared oracle. Expected values are POSIX's own requirement -- the exit status, and whether anything was echoed -- read off a live fast-backend run.
  PASS .SILENT silences the whole makefile -- If the special target .SILENT is specified without prerequisites, commands shall not be written.
       hand-authored: runs a real recipe: the command differs by system, so there is no shared oracle. Expected values are POSIX's own requirement -- the exit status, and whether anything was echoed -- read off a live fast-backend run.
  PASS a failing command stops the build, exit 1 -- If a command returns a non-zero status, make shall terminate.
       hand-authored: runs a real recipe: the command differs by system, so there is no shared oracle. Expected values are POSIX's own requirement -- the exit status, and whether anything was echoed -- read off a live fast-backend run.
  PASS -i ignores a failing command -- -i  Ignore error codes returned by invoked commands.
       hand-authored: runs a real recipe: the command differs by system, so there is no shared oracle. Expected values are POSIX's own requirement -- the exit status, and whether anything was echoed -- read off a live fast-backend run.
  PASS .IGNORE ignores a failing command -- If the special target .IGNORE is specified without prerequisites, error codes shall be ignored.
       hand-authored: runs a real recipe: the command differs by system, so there is no shared oracle. Expected values are POSIX's own requirement -- the exit status, and whether anything was echoed -- read off a live fast-backend run.
  PASS a - prefix ignores that one command -- If the command prefix contains a hyphen ... any error found while executing the command shall be ignored.
       hand-authored: runs a real recipe: the command differs by system, so there is no shared oracle. Expected values are POSIX's own requirement -- the exit status, and whether anything was echoed -- read off a live fast-backend run.
  PASS -k builds the other branch and still exits 1 -- -k  Continue to update other targets that do not depend on the current target if a non-ignored error occurs.
       hand-authored: runs a real recipe: the command differs by system, so there is no shared oracle. Expected values are POSIX's own requirement -- the exit status, and whether anything was echoed -- read off a live fast-backend run.
  PASS without -k the second branch is never reached -- Commands returning nonzero status cause make to terminate.
       hand-authored: runs a real recipe: the command differs by system, so there is no shared oracle. Expected values are POSIX's own requirement -- the exit status, and whether anything was echoed -- read off a live fast-backend run.
  PASS -p writes the macro and target database -- -p  Write to standard output the complete set of macro definitions and target descriptions.
       hand-authored: -p has no specified format. POSIX asks for "the complete set of macro definitions and target descriptions"; this is what make.s prints, and the row pins it so the shape cannot drift unnoticed.
  PASS a target with no rule and no file is an error -- If no rule and no file exist for a target, make shall report an error.
       hand-authored: runs a real recipe: the command differs by system, so there is no shared oracle. Expected values are POSIX's own requirement -- the exit status, and whether anything was echoed -- read off a live fast-backend run.
  PASS a circular dependency is reported, not looped on -- make shall not loop on a circular dependency.
       hand-authored: runs a real recipe: the command differs by system, so there is no shared oracle. Expected values are POSIX's own requirement -- the exit status, and whether anything was echoed -- read off a live fast-backend run.

== man (6 cases) ==
  PASS no such page: a message and exit 1 -- man: no manual entry for NAME
       hand-authored: man.s's own :noentry path writes to fd 2 (not captured by this harness) and exits 1; stdout is empty either way.
  PASS a leading numeric section is accepted and ignored -- man [section] name
       hand-authored: proves the section operand was consumed as a section, not folded into the page name -- the diagnostic (fd 2, uncaptured) still names 'nosuchpage', and the exit status is the same 'no manual entry' 1, not a usage error.
  PASS more than two operands is a usage error -- man [section] name
       hand-authored: man.s's own :usage path, exit 2 by house convention.
  PASS an operand beginning with - is a usage error -- man [section] name
       hand-authored: -k/-f (apropos/whatis) are KNOWN_GAPS -- this tree builds no page index to search (man.s's own header) -- so man.s refuses any '-'-leading operand as a usage error rather than silently ignoring it.
  PASS a not-applicable stub page renders and exits 0 -- man [section] name
       hand-authored: docs/man/ar.1's own DESCRIPTION names /LIB/LIBK, the library directory ld(1) links from instead of an archive format -- that fragment can only reach stdout if the stub page's real text was rendered and copied through, proving the not-applicable stub added 2026-09-17 actually pages.
  PASS an existing real page still renders and exits 0 -- man [section] name
       hand-authored: docs/man/tty.1 names /dev/ttya0 (the SSC line) in its DESCRIPTION -- proving man's ordinary success path (a real, non-stub page) still works after the stub pages were added, not just the not-applicable row above.

== mkdir (5 cases) ==
  PASS -p accepts an existing directory leaf -- Each dir operand that names an existing directory shall be ignored without error.
  PASS -p creates missing intermediate components -- -p -- Create any missing intermediate pathname components.
       hand-authored: the assertion is a filesystem side effect (the whole A/B/C chain really exists and is open()-able), proven by a marker WRITE this harness makes into the new leaf directory after mkdir returns -- there is no tool stdout to diff against a host oracle for that; the expected marker content is this harness's own choice of literal, not a value captured from a maux run.
  PASS without -p, a missing parent fails -- >0 An error occurred.
  PASS success is exit 0 -- 0 All the specified directories were created successfully...
  GAP  -m mode is unimplemented -- -m mode -- Set the file permission bits of the newly-created directory to the specified mode value.
       known gap: mkdir.s's OPTLIST is the single byte "p" (grep OPTLIST in src/bin/mkdir.s) -- '-m' is not a registered option at all, so GETOPT reports it bad and mkdir.s takes the :usage exit-2 path before ever reaching a dir operand. BSD mkdir -m 755 creates the directory with that mode and exits 0.

== mv (4 cases) ==
  PASS mv renames within the same directory -- shall perform actions equivalent to the rename() function
       hand-authored: side effect verified via a follow-up CAT in the same session; see cp -r's own reason.
  PASS -i declines overwrite on end of input -- Prompt for confirmation if the destination path exists.
       hand-authored: the two follow-up CATs prove a declined overwrite left both operands unchanged.
  PASS -f replaces without prompting -- Do not prompt for confirmation if the destination path exists.
       hand-authored: the follow-up CAT proves replacement.
  PASS -i affirmative response replaces destination -- If the response is affirmative, mv shall perform the move.
       hand-authored: the follow-up CAT proves the affirmative response permitted replacement.

== nl (23 cases) ==
  PASS default: only non-empty body lines are numbered, width 6, right justified, no leading zeros, one tab -- -b type ... The default type ... shall be t.
  PASS -b a numbers every line, including an empty one -- -b type ... a ... Number all lines.
  PASS -b n numbers no lines at all (still prints the blank field and separator) -- -b type ... n ... No lines shall be numbered.
       hand-authored: the XCU text does not say what an unnumbered line carries in place of its number; BSD nl prints the blank field then the separator (what nl.s does), GNU nl prints the blank field then the separator as spaces. No host is the oracle here.
  PASS -n rz right-justifies and zero-pads to the field width -- -n format ... rz ... Line numbers shall be ... right-justified ... with leading zeros.
  PASS -s sep sets the text between the number and the line -- -s string ... Add string as a character(s) that shall be used in place of the default <tab>.
  PASS -v/-i set the start value and the increment -- -v line_number ... -i incr ...
  PASS a delimiter line switches sections and is itself consumed, never printed or numbered -- a header ... shall begin with a line containing only the string \:\:\: ...
       hand-authored: the XCU text says a delimiter line signals a section start and nothing about output; BSD nl emits nothing for it (what nl.s does), GNU nl emits an empty line. No host is the oracle here.
  PASS -l num (adjacent blank lines counted as one) -- -l num -- Specify the number of blank lines to be considered as one. For example, -l 2 results in only the second adjacent blank line being numbered
       hand-authored: SHIPPED 2026-09-17: BLANKCNT (nlfmt.s's PRINTONE) counts blank lines within the current run under -b a; only every NLNUM'th one is numbered. Verified live against this Mac's own nl before writing the mechanism (file header) -- the SAME grouping rule, since POSIX's own text ('only the second adjacent blank line being numbered') is unambiguous and BSD/GNU agree on it, unlike the surrounding format.
  PASS more than one file operand is a usage error -- SYNOPSIS -- nl ... [file]
       hand-authored: nl.s's own usage-error path -- exit 2 is this tree's uniform usage-error convention. This host's own nl also refuses a second operand, but with a different diagnostic wording and its own exit status -- not host-oracled because of that mismatch, only the maux-side behavior is asserted here.
  PASS a header, body, or footer delimiter ALIKE resets the counter unless -p is given -- -p ... do not reset the line number at the start of a logical page.
       hand-authored: measured live against this host's own /usr/bin/nl (see nl.s's own header): H1/H2 print with a BLANK field (default -h n -- header lines are not numbered, but they still print) and the SECOND page's own header delimiter resets the counter to 1 too, not only the first page's -- the XCU prose alone reads as header-only, so this row pins the measured behavior rather than the narrower reading.
  PASS pBRE: a BRE that no line matches numbers nothing (formerly the KNOWN GAP row) -- -b type ... pBRE ... Number only lines that contain a match for the basic regular expression bre.
       hand-authored: RETIRED 2026-10-08, not deleted: this row was the known gap (exit 2, no engine linked) and is now the no-match case. Both hosts print every line unnumbered; the prefix shape is the BSD one (see the pBRE block comment above).
  PASS pBRE: -b pfoo numbers exactly the lines containing foo -- -b type ... pBRE ... Number only lines that contain a match for the basic regular expression bre.
       hand-authored: unanchored substring match, numbering continues across the selected lines only. Measured on the Mac (BSD) and the box (GNU): same selection and numbers; the two differ only in the unnumbered-line prefix.
  PASS pBRE: the BRE follows the p with no separator (-bpfoo) -- -b type ... The p ... shall be followed by the BRE
       hand-authored: the glued spelling is the same option; measured identical on both hosts.
  PASS pBRE: ^ and $ anchor the BRE to the line -- -b type ... pBRE ... basic regular expression bre.
       hand-authored: ^f.*d$ matches only food: foo does not end in d, and foo bar does not.
  PASS pBRE: a group and a backreference -- -b type ... pBRE ... basic regular expression bre.
       hand-authored: the group and \1 spell "oo": a BRE feature the engine owes POSIX. Same selection on BSD and GNU.
  PASS pBRE: a bracket expression selects -- -b type ... pBRE ... basic regular expression bre.
       hand-authored: two adjacent vowels: three (ee), foo, food, foo bar. Same selection on BSD and GNU.
  PASS pBRE: ^$ numbers only the empty line -- -b type ... pBRE ... Number only lines that contain a match for the basic regular expression bre.
       hand-authored: THE ORACLES DISAGREE and the BSD one is the wrong one: this Mac numbers ALL EIGHT lines for p^$, the box (GNU) numbers only the empty line. ^$ matches an empty line and nothing else in POSIX BRE, which is GNU's answer and the engine's.
  PASS pBRE: an empty BRE matches every line -- -b type ... pBRE ... (the XCU text says nothing about an empty bre)
       hand-authored: unspecified by XCU. GNU numbers every line, the empty one included; BSD refuses with "empty (sub)expression". maux follows GNU: an empty pattern matches at every line start, which is what the engine returns for it.
  PASS pBRE: -n, -w and -s still shape the number field -- -b type ... pBRE; -n format; -s string; -w number
       hand-authored: BSD shape for the unnumbered line (blank field then the separator itself); the numbered lines agree on both hosts.
  PASS pBRE: a FOREIGN low-ASCII file selects the same lines -- -b type ... pBRE ... Number only lines that contain a match for the basic regular expression bre.
       hand-authored: the file arrives as ProDOS tools write it -- LOW ASCII, $0D -- not as maux does (high bit, $8D). The engine reads bit 7 SET, so NLMATCH matches a normalised copy (grepbody.s NORMLINE's rule, learning:01M28FXZHBN4V6T4NT6PZZ4M4S is the sibling arena finding); a tool that matched the raw bytes would number nothing. Staged raw by stage_batch, hand= only -- the host oracle never sees a bytes file.
  PASS pBRE: -h selects header lines -- -h type ... pBRE ...
       hand-authored: the header delimiter is consumed (BSD prints nothing for it, GNU prints an empty line -- the delimiter-row split above); the numbering agrees on both hosts.
  PASS pBRE: -f selects footer lines -- -f type ... pBRE ...
       hand-authored: the footer twin of the -h row; same split over the delimiter line, same numbering.
  PASS pBRE: each section keeps its OWN pattern (-p, no reset) -- -h type -b type -f type; -p ... Do not restart the numbering of lines at logical page delimiters.
       hand-authored: three patterns, one per section: header and footer number *foo*, body numbers *bar*. -p keeps the count running so the row does not depend on the reset policy (BSD resets at headers only, GNU at every delimiter, nl.s at every delimiter). Measured on both hosts: same selection and numbers.

== nohup (4 cases) ==
  PASS pass-through: a quick utility (ECHO), exit 0 -- the SIGHUP signal ... shall be set to be ignored ... utility shall be invoked
       hand-authored: stdout is redirected to a real file here, never a tty -- nohup's own SC_FDSTAT check finds no terminal and never touches fd 1/2 or creates NOHUP.OUT, so this is a pure pass-through of ECHO's own output and exit status.
  PASS pass-through: a failing utility (FALSE), exit 1 -- the exit status of nohup shall be that of the utility specified by the utility operand
       hand-authored: FALSE has no stdout; its own exit status (1) passes straight through SC_WAIT, untouched (env.s's own convention this file's header cites).
  PASS utility not found: exit 127 -- 127 An error occurred in the nohup utility or the utility specified by the utility operand could not be found
       hand-authored: PATHRSLV fails on every PATHTAB entry -- the same 127 env.s/time.s already use for a not-found child.
  PASS no operand is a usage error -- nohup utility [argument...]
       hand-authored: this tree's own uniform usage-error convention (comm.s/cmp.s/etc: exit 2); GNU and BSD nohup each print their own usage text and may use a different code for a missing operand, so this stays hand-authored rather than host-oracled.

== od (55 cases) ==
  PASS default is hex bytes (od.s ruling, not V6 octal words) -- -x Interpret words (two-byte units) in hexadecimal.
       hand-authored: od.s's default is HEX BYTES by ruling (docs/ANACHRONISMS.md A3, the file's own header: a 6502 byte machine, not a PDP-11 with octal front-panel words) -- a structural default divergence from BSD/GNU od (octal 16-bit words, octal offset), not a value to translate flags around. Hand-computed from the fixture's own on-disk bytes via od_expected(), which mirrors od.s's row printers exactly; not a host capture.
