== pathchk (9 cases) ==
  PASS a good name passes with no diagnostic -- 0 ... every pathname was ... valid.
  PASS a 16-char component exceeds this tree's 15-char limit -- >0 An error occurred.
       hand-authored: ProDOS's own directory-entry name_length field is 4 bits wide (15 max, PC_COMPMAX in src/bin/pathchk.s) -- this host's own pathchk has no such limit for the DEFAULT check (NAME_MAX is filesystem-dependent and far larger; verified live: this exact 16-character name passes on this Mac, rc=0).
  PASS -P flags a component beginning with a hyphen -- -P ... a pathname component that begins with a <hyphen-minus> ... shall generate a diagnostic
  PASS -P flags an empty pathname -- -P ... an empty string ... shall generate a diagnostic
  PASS -p passes a 14-char portable component, the exact limit -- -p ... _POSIX_NAME_MAX (14) ...
  PASS -p a 15-char component exceeds the portable limit by one -- -p ... _POSIX_NAME_MAX (14) ...
  PASS -p a space is outside the portable character set -- -p ... the portable filename character set ...
  PASS a nonexistent directory prefix is not an error -- It is not considered an error if a pathname argument contains a nonexistent component as long as a component by that name could be created.
       hand-authored: the XCU's own sentence, quoted above: a MISSING intermediate directory is not a failure on either side (verified live against this host's own pathchk, rc=0) -- hand-authored anyway because pathchk.s's own default-mode length/charset checks still run over 'nosuchdir123'/'leaf' (both comfortably inside this tree's 15/64 limits), so the row is really pinning that existence and syntax are checked independently, not just replaying a host measurement that happens to agree.
  PASS several operands: one fails under -p, all are still checked -- >0 An error occurred.

== pr (74 cases) ==
  PASS -t suppresses header/trailer/pagination entirely -- -t ... Write neither the five-line identifying header ... nor the five-line trailer ...
  PASS -t -n numbers each line, default width 5 and a tab -- -n[char][width] ... provide width digit line numbering ...
  PASS -t -n:3 sets an explicit separator and width -- -n[char][width] ... char ... width ...
  PASS -t -d double-spaces the content -- -d ... Produce output that is double spaced ...
  PASS -t -o 4 indents every line by 4 spaces -- -o offset ... Each line of output shall be preceded by offset <space> characters ...
  PASS -l 10 on a short file falls back to the -t shape -- -l lines ... Override the 66-line default ...
       hand-authored: a page length of 10 leaves no room for even one content line under a 5-line header and a same-size trailer (BODYCAP = lines-10 = 0), so pr.s falls back to the -t shape entirely -- matching this host's own BSD pr EXACTLY at this boundary (measured live: `pr -l 10` on the identical 15-line file prints no header at all, just the 15 lines straight through, rc=0) -- not host-oracled only because the harness's own comparator re-runs the host oracle live rather than trusting a single captured measurement, and this exact edge is cheaper to pin by hand than to re-justify every run.
  PASS -h replaces the filename in the header -- -h header ... Use header ... in place of the pathname ...
       hand-authored: the header's own date is real wall-clock time and cannot be byte-matched across runs (see this table's own header); this row overwrites OUT with GREP -c's count of the TITLE string -h's own text puts in the header, proving the substitution happened without asserting the date around it.
  PASS +page skips every page before N -- +page ... Begin output at page number page ...
       hand-authored: 60 lines is exactly two default (66-line) pages; +2 skips page 1's header/body/trailer entirely while still consuming its input (page 2's own content is lines 57-60, unaffected). Overwrites OUT with two GREP -c counts -- 'Page 2' appears once, 'Page 1' appears zero times -- proving the skip without the date.
  PASS multiple FILE operands each start their own page 1 -- pr ... file ...
       hand-authored: each file operand gets its own independent pagination (measured live against this host's own BSD pr): 'Page 1' appears once per file, twice total, proving the reset without the date.
  PASS no file operand reads standard input -- If no file operands are specified, ... the standard input shall be used.
       hand-authored: proves pr ran its normal paginated path on stdin (one page, no crash on the blank filename field) without asserting the date.
  PASS -column multi-column output (down, then across) -- -column ... The number of columns of text to write on each page ...
  PASS -m merge: one column per file -- -m ... Merge files, one per column ...
  PASS -a across-column ordering -- -a ... Modify the effect of the - column option ...
  PASS -F form-feed page separator -- -F ... Use a <form-feed> ... rather than the default ...
  PASS -e input tab expansion -- -e[char][gap] ... Expand ... <tab> characters ...
  PASS -i output tab compression -- -i[char][gap] ... replace <space> characters with <tab> characters ...
  PASS -s column separator -- -s[char] ... separate text columns by ... char ...
  PASS -t -3 fills columns down (3 x 3) -- -column ... written down each column in the order in which the text is received
  PASS -t -2 down, uneven halves (4 + 3) -- -column ... When used with -t, use the minimum number of lines to write the output.
  PASS -t -a -3 fills rows across (3 x 3) -- -a ... Modify the effect of the -column option so that the columns are filled across the page
  PASS -t -a -3 with a short last row -- -a ... filled across the page in a round-robin order
  PASS -t -3 -a given in the other order -- -a ... Modify the effect of the -column option
  PASS -t -5 -w 20: cells are 3 wide (text width rule) -- -w width ... Set the width of the line to width column positions for multiple text-column output only.
  PASS -t -3 -w 30 truncates every cell, last column too -- -column ... lines longer than the column are truncated
  PASS -t -2 -w 30 truncates (two columns, four lines) -- -w width ...
  PASS -t -3 text of 22, 23, 24 chars: the tab-stop boundary -- -column ... column width (line width - separators) / columns
  PASS -t -2 text of 35 and 36 chars: the cell boundary -- -column ...
  PASS -t -3 -s: separator replaces the padding -- -s[char] ... Separate text columns by the single character char instead of the appropriate number of <space>s
  PASS -t -3 -s alone separates with a tab -- -s[char] ... (default for char is the <tab> character)
  PASS -t -a -2 -s, across with a separator -- -s[char] ...
  PASS -t -3 -d double-spaces the rows -- -d ... Produce output that is double spaced.
       hand-authored: a blank line follows EVERY row, the last included (BSD, and this tree's single-column -d); GNU leaves out the one after the last row (measured 2026-10-08 on coreutils 9.1), so neither host can be the oracle for both rigs.
  PASS -t -3 -o 4 indents every row -- -o offset ...
  PASS -t -3 -l 3: pages of three rows, exact fit (18 lines) -- -l lines ... text columns never exceed the page length
  PASS -t -3 -l 3: one line over a full page (10 lines) -- -l lines ...
  PASS -t -3 reads standard input -- If no file operands are specified, ... the standard input shall be used.
  PASS -t -3 with an empty file prints nothing -- -column ...
  PASS -t -1 is the single-column shape -- -column ... (the default shall be 1)
  PASS -3 -l 20: 31 lines, a full page and a one-line page -- -column ... -l lines ...
  PASS -a -3 -l 20: across, two pages -- -a ...
  PASS -2 -d -l 14: double-spaced rows, two pages -- -d ...
  PASS -3 -l 11: one row per page -- -l lines ...
  PASS -3 -l 10 falls back to the -t shape -- -l lines ...
  PASS -t -a -2 -o 3: the offset is in every row -- -o offset ...
  PASS -F -l 12 replaces the page padding with a form feed -- -F ... Use a <form-feed> character for new pages, instead of the default behavior that uses a sequence of <newline> characters.
  PASS -F ends an exactly full page with its form feed -- -F ...
  PASS -F -3 -l 20: columns and form feeds -- -F ...
  PASS -t -F writes no form feed (no pages) -- -F ... -t ...
       hand-authored: -t turns pagination off, so there are no page boundaries to put a form feed at. GNU agrees; BSD writes a form feed after the last line anyway (measured 2026-10-08), so neither host can be the oracle for both rigs.
  PASS -t -e expands tabs to every 8th column -- -e[char][gap] ... default gap 8
  PASS -t -e4 expands to every 4th column -- -e[char][gap] ...
  PASS -t without -e leaves a tab alone -- -e ... (not assumed for a single column)
  PASS -t -i contracts runs of spaces to tabs -- -i[char][gap] ...
  PASS -t -i drops trailing spaces -- -i[char][gap] ...
  PASS -t -i3 contracts to every 3rd column -- -i[char][gap] ...
  PASS -t -i: uses the given character instead of a tab -- -i[char][gap] ...
  PASS -t -e -i together -- -e ... -i ...
  PASS -t -3 -e expands before the columns are cut -- -e ... Expand ... before ... the columns
  PASS -t -m two files, equal length -- -m ... Merge files, one per column
  PASS -t -m three files -- -m ...
  PASS -t -m -s: separator -- -s[char] ...
  PASS -t -m -w 30 truncates each cell -- -w width ...
  PASS -t -m -n numbers the merged rows once -- -n ... -m
       hand-authored: one number per merged row, before the first file's text. The second column stays at its own column start (72 / 2 = 36); BSD starts it at 37 and GNU at 40 (measured 2026-10-08), so neither is the oracle.
  PASS -m -l 12: merged pages of two rows -- -m ... -l lines ...
  PASS -t -m with five files (the cap) -- -m ...
  PASS -t -m reads standard input for the - operand -- A <hyphen-minus> ... shall be taken to be the standard input
  PASS -m refuses -column (exit 1, nothing written) -- -m ... This option should not be used with -column
  PASS -t -3 over ten lines: columns are filled left first -- -column ... Whether or not text columns are produced with identical vertical lengths is unspecified
       hand-authored: XCU leaves the balance of a short last page unspecified, and the hosts differ: BSD fills the left columns first (4, 4, 2 -- this row), GNU balances (4, 3, 3). Both use 4 rows. maux follows BSD; the host-oracled rows above use fixtures both agree on.
  PASS -t -m with a short file: its cell is absent, the rest keep their column -- -m ... Merge files, one per column
       hand-authored: XCU does not say what -m writes for a file that has run out. BSD stops the row after the last present cell (this row); GNU pads the row to full width with trailing tabs/spaces. maux follows BSD.
  PASS -t -m with a short MIDDLE file keeps the later column -- -m ...
       hand-authored: measured on BSD: an absent middle cell still occupies its column, so the third file's text stays at its own column start (width 72 / 3 = 24: 'b' then 23 spaces to column 48 = tabs at 8, 16, 24, 32, 40, 48 -> six tabs).
  PASS -t -n -3 numbers every cell (XCU), tab after the number -- -n[char][width] ... In multi-column output the number occupies the first width positions of each column
       hand-authored: GNU's layout (and the XCU's wording: each column of text is numbered). BSD pads the number differently after the first column ('      5 d'), so it cannot be the oracle.
  PASS -t -3 -s with -w: the cell width follows the XCU rule -- -s[char] ... -w width ...
       hand-authored: width 30, 3 columns, 2 separators: (30 - 2) / 3 = 9 text columns (the XCU rule; BSD agrees). GNU keeps 10 under -s, so it cannot be the oracle.
  PASS a column page over the line table fails loudly -- maux limit: one page of columns holds at most 1024 lines
       hand-authored: 250 rows x 8 columns = 2000 lines per page, over the 1024-line table: exit 1 with a message, nothing written. A host prints the two lines.
  PASS -m with six files fails cleanly (cap is five) -- maux limit: -m takes at most 5 files
       hand-authored: a process has 8 fd-table entries (PCB_FDTABLE) and stdin, stdout and stderr hold three, so five FILE operands is what fits; the sixth is refused BEFORE anything is opened or written: exit 1, nothing on stdout. A host merges all six. ('-' is stdin and costs no slot.)
  PASS -w 255 without -s is refused (columns are 8-bit) -- maux limit: columns are tracked in 8 bits
       hand-authored: -w 255 without -s: exit 1 with a message, nothing written (documented in the man page). -s lifts the limit because separated columns need no padding.
  PASS -w 300 does not fit a number and is a usage error -- maux limit: option numbers are 8-bit
       hand-authored: PARSEDEC reports a value past 255 instead of wrapping it (300 used to read as 44): usage error, exit 2, nothing written. A host accepts it.

== printf (18 cases) ==
  PASS a plain %s conversion, reused across arguments -- the format operand shall be reused as often as necessary to satisfy the argument operands
  PASS %d/%x/%o with field widths and flags -- -, 0: the conversion specifications in the C language.
  PASS %s precision truncates, width pads -- A field precision ... specifies the maximum number of characters to be written.
  PASS %c prints only the first character -- c -- The first character of argument shall be printed.
  PASS %c of an empty/missing argument prints nothing -- the corresponding argument ... shall be treated as ... the null string
       hand-authored: The two hosts DISAGREE: BSD printf (this Mac) prints nothing for %c of the null string, GNU coreutils printf (the Linux box) emits a NUL byte (it hands *argument, i.e. the terminating zero, to the C library %c). The XCU text says the first character of a null string -- there is none -- so nothing is printed; the spec is the oracle here, not whichever host runs the sweep (first seen 2026-09-14, box sweep of 6eaba19, 299/300).
  PASS %% is a literal percent, no argument consumed -- '%%' -- Print a '%' character.
  PASS backslash escapes in the format: \011 (tab) -- \t' Write a <tab> character.
  PASS \ddd: 1-3 octal digits, no leading 0 needed -- '\ddd' -- Write a byte whose value is the 1, 2, or 3-digit octal number ddd.
  PASS %b interprets its OWN backslash escapes -- b -- ... <backslash> characters shall be recognized and removed; any special meaning shall be applied.
  PASS %b's \c stops all remaining output -- '\c' -- Ignore the remaining characters in the string operand containing it, any remaining string operands, and any additional characters in the format operand.
  PASS format reused, missing arguments are empty/0 -- if the format requires more arguments than there are argument operands ... the extra conversion specifications ... behave as if ... a null string argument.
  PASS a non-numeric numeric argument: diagnosed, 0 used -- If the argument cannot be entirely converted into an internal value ... a diagnostic message shall be written ... and the utility shall not exit with a zero exit status
       hand-authored: check_posixutil's oracle never captures stderr (module header), so the diagnostic wording -- not maux's -- never enters the comparison; only stdout ("0") and the exit status (1) are checked, both verified live against this host's own /usr/bin/printf.
  PASS a leading quote argument takes the char's value -- If the leading character is a single-quote or double-quote, the value shall be the numeric value in the underlying codeset of the character following the single-quote or double-quote.
  PASS no format operand at all is a usage error -- printf format [argument...]
       hand-authored: POSIX gives no exit status for this case; maux's own usage message goes to stderr (not compared here) and it exits 1, the same posture every other util's usage error takes in this suite.
  GAP  %e is unimplemented (no floating point) -- e, E -- ... the argument shall be written in the style [-]d.ddde+-dd.
       known gap: This machine has no floating point (src/bin/printf.s's own header); %e falls through to the unknown-conversion diagnostic + BADNUM, unlike a host printf which formats a real float.
  GAP  %f is unimplemented (no floating point) -- f, F -- ... the argument shall be written in ... decimal notation.
       known gap: Same reason as %e -- no floating point on this machine.
  GAP  %g is unimplemented (no floating point) -- g, G -- ... the argument shall be written in style e/E or f/F.
       known gap: Same reason as %e -- no floating point on this machine.
  GAP  %a is unimplemented (no floating point) -- a, A -- ... the argument shall be written in ... hexadecimal notation.
       known gap: Same reason as %e -- no floating point on this machine.

== pwd (1 cases) ==
  PASS prints the working directory -- "%s\n", <directory pathname>
       hand-authored: pwd prints a ProDOS prefix, which has no relationship to the host's own cwd -- there is no host argv this could possibly share. ProDOS prefixes are absolute with a trailing slash (structural fact, not a maux-run capture, and the same convention independently pinned by scripts/build_v1_system_image.py's EXPECT_PWD1 and check_play.py's own PWD case); the expected VALUE is this harness's own per-case /mnt/MAUX/PXUT/<K-tag> directory, since run_case() cd's into it before typing any measured command, pwd included.

== realpath (11 cases) ==
  PASS a plain file resolves to <cwd>/<name> -- canonicalize the specified pathname
       hand-authored: base is this case's own per-run directory (check_posixutil.py's own maux_mapping()), exactly what GET_PREFIX reports as cwd -- no host process shares this ProDOS path space to compare against.
  PASS ./A/../F folds through a subdirectory and back -- does not contain any components that are dot or dot-dot
       hand-authored: RP_SPLIT folds the leading '.' and the 'A/..' pair away entirely -- the result is identical to the plain-file row above, proving the fold rather than just the pass-through.
  PASS an absolute operand is returned unchanged -- canonicalize the specified pathname
       hand-authored: $(pwd) is maux's OWN shell substituting its current directory before REALPATH ever runs, so the operand is already absolute -- RP_RESOLVE's leading-'/' branch, no cwd lookup at all.
  PASS -E allows the last component to be missing (default) -- -E Do not treat it as an error if ... the last component ... results in ... [ENOENT]
       hand-authored: NOSUCH is never staged; -E checks only the parent (this case's own directory, which does exist) and prints the folded path regardless of the last component's own existence.
  PASS -e requires the last component to exist -- -e Treat it as an error if ... the last component ... results in ... [ENOENT]
       hand-authored: the same missing NOSUCH as the -E row above, but -e checks the FULL path including the last component -- GET_FILE_INFO fails, nothing is printed, exit 1.
  PASS a missing middle component fails under the default (-E) -- -E ... [does not exempt] errors other than [ENOENT] on the last component
       hand-authored: -E's own parent check is on NOSUCHDIR (the component BEFORE the last, F), which does not exist -- ENOENT on a non-last component is not the exemption -E grants, so this fails exactly as -e would.
  PASS a missing middle component fails under -e too -- -e Treat it as an error
       hand-authored: same missing NOSUCHDIR as the default row above; -e's own full-path check fails the same way, same exit 1 -- this row exists to show the -E/-e distinction does not matter here, not to find a new failure mode.
  PASS two operands, one fails: the good one still prints, exit 1 -- a pathname to be canonicalized
       hand-authored: each operand is resolved independently (RP_EXIT accumulates failures but the loop never stops) -- F succeeds and prints before NOSUCHDIR/X is even reached, then that second operand fails and sets the overall exit status to 1 without erasing what F already printed.
  PASS a directory operand resolves the same as a file -- canonicalize the specified pathname
       hand-authored: realpath does not distinguish a directory operand from a file one -- GET_FILE_INFO succeeds on either storage type, and RP_CHECK never inspects STYP at all.
  PASS .. above the volume root stays at the root -- canonicalize ... does not contain any components that are dot or dot-dot
       hand-authored: this suite's own base is 3 components below the carrier volume (/mnt/MAUX/PXUT/<K-tag>); 4 levels of '..' pop the K-tag, PXUT, the volume and then /mnt itself, which lands on the kernel's virtual root (docs/VROOT.md, volumes live under /mnt). It used to stop at the volume name; the volume is now a directory like any other.
  PASS .. above the virtual root stays at the root -- canonicalize ... does not contain any components that are dot or dot-dot
       hand-authored: one more '..' than the row above: the root has no parent, so realpath stays on '/' (liba MKP_POP refuses to pop it), the same answer a POSIX realpath gives.

== rm (4 cases) ==
  PASS rm -r removes a directory tree -- Remove file hierarchies.
       hand-authored: if 'D' really is gone, a plain MKDIR D (no -p) recreates it successfully (exit 0); if rm -r left anything behind, MKDIR D fails (D already exists), exit >0 -- a side-effect proof, hand-authored for the same reason as cp -r's own case.
  PASS -f forgives a nonexistent operand, exit 0 -- Do not write diagnostic messages or modify the exit status in the case of ... operands that do not exist.
  PASS -i declines removal on end of input -- Prompt for confirmation as described previously.
       hand-authored: the follow-up CAT proves a declined removal left the operand intact.
  PASS -i affirmative response removes operand -- If the response is affirmative, rm shall attempt to remove it.
       hand-authored: MKDIR F succeeds only if rm removed the file.

== rmdir (8 cases) ==
  PASS removes an empty directory, exit 0 -- The rmdir utility shall remove the directory entry specified by each dir operand.
  PASS a non-empty directory is an error -- >0 An error occurred.
  PASS a missing directory is an error -- >0 An error occurred.
  PASS -p removes every pathname component in turn -- -p ... effects equivalent to the following command shall occur: rmdir -p $(dirname dir)
  PASS -p side effect: the WHOLE chain is gone, not just the leaf -- -p ... effects equivalent to the following command shall occur: rmdir -p $(dirname dir)
       hand-authored: side effect verified via a follow-up MKDIR in the SAME shell session (mkdir.s's own '-p creates missing intermediate components' precedent, module header's own reasoning) -- there is no tool stdout to diff a filesystem side effect against; if 'A' really is gone all the way up, a plain MKDIR A (no -p) recreates it successfully (exit 0); if the walk stopped early, MKDIR A fails (A still exists), exit >0.
  PASS -p stops at the first non-empty ancestor -- -p ... effects equivalent to the following command shall occur: rmdir -p $(dirname dir)
  PASS no operand is a usage error -- >0 An error occurred.
       hand-authored: rmdir.s's own :usage path, mkdir.s's identical shape -- exit 2 is this tree's uniform usage-error convention, which POSIX's generic '>0' permits. This host's own rmdir with no operand prints its own usage banner and exits 1 -- a different but also nonzero exit, not host-oracled because of that mismatch.
  PASS a failed operand does not stop the remaining ones -- rmdir shall remove the directory entry specified by each dir operand
       hand-authored: side effect verified via a follow-up MKDIR in the same session -- if D really was removed despite NOSUCH's earlier failure, a plain MKDIR D (no -p) recreates it successfully (exit 0); if the loop stopped at the first failure, D still exists and MKDIR D fails, exit >0. Overall exit 1 either way (NOSUCH alone already fails the run), which is why stdout/rc cannot distinguish the two paths on their own.

