== timeout (11 cases) ==
  PASS a quick utility exits with its own status (TRUE) -- otherwise, the exit status of timeout shall be that of utility
       hand-authored: TRUE returns well inside the 5 s deadline; no signal is ever sent, so timeout's own exit is TRUE's own status, unchanged.
  PASS a quick failing utility still exits with its own status (FALSE) -- otherwise, the exit status of timeout shall be that of utility
       hand-authored: same reasoning, FALSE's 1.
  PASS a utility that outruns duration is killed, exit 124 -- 124 The utility named by utility timed out
       hand-authored: SLEEP has no stdout. The 1 s deadline fires well before SLEEP's own 3 s is up; timeout sends the default TERM (kill-signo-abi: DFL disposition terminates unconditionally, no cooperation needed from SLEEP), reaps it, and reports 124 -- -p was not given, so the child's own $80|signo status is NOT preserved.
  PASS -p preserves the child status after a timeout -- -p ... the exit status shall be that of utility
       hand-authored: 143 = $8F = $80|15 -- SLEEP's own wait status after the default TERM, the same kill-signo-abi encoding kill.s's own header documents ($8F TERM). -p reports this instead of the bare 124.
  PASS -s selects the signal by name (KILL) -- -s signal ... send that signal ... instead of ... SIGTERM
       hand-authored: signo 9 (KILL, shtrap.s's own name table -- see this tool's own header for why this is NOT kill.s's mechanism) force-kills unconditionally; the exit code is still 124 without -p regardless of which signal fired.
  PASS -k follows a signal with a grace period -- -k time ... send a KILL signal to the utility if it is still running
       hand-authored: SLEEP has no disposition of its own (SIGA_DFL) so the FIRST signal (default TERM) already terminates it -- the -k grace path (KTARGET computed, the poll continues) is exercised but its own follow-up KILL is never NEEDED to fire. Nothing in this tree's fleet arms SIGA_IGN for itself, so a row that forces the follow-up KILL to actually fire cannot be built from existing utilities; this row proves -k does not break the ordinary case instead.
  PASS a bad duration is a usage error, exit 125 -- 125 if the duration parameter or the timeout utility itself is invalid
       hand-authored: TO_PARSEDUR's first non-digit character ('a') matches neither a digit nor s/m/h -- bad duration, refused before any spawn.
  PASS a fractional duration (whole seconds) -- a decimal integer ... optionally followed by ... s, m, h, or d
       hand-authored: SHIPPED 2026-09-17: TO_PARSEDUR now takes ONE fractional digit when the unit resolves to seconds -- '1.5' is 90 ticks (60 ticks/s / 10 tenths = 6 ticks/tenth, times 5). TRUE exits before the 90-tick deadline either way, so this row proves the duration PARSED (no usage-error 125) rather than the timing itself -- same shape as this module's other '-k does not break the ordinary case' row.
  PASS a fractional minute is a duration -- a decimal integer ... optionally followed by ... s, m, h, or d
       hand-authored: '1.5m' is 5400 ticks: 1 minute (3600) plus five tenths of a minute (5 x 360). TRUE exits long before that either way, so this row proves the duration PARSED -- no usage-error 125 -- which is exactly what the refusal it replaces used to produce.
  PASS no utility operand is a usage error, exit 125 -- 125 if ... timeout did not have enough information
       hand-authored: fewer than two operands remain past options -- refused before any spawn, same lane as the bad-duration row above.
  PASS utility not found: exit 127 -- 126/127 as nohup
       hand-authored: PATHRSLV fails on every PATHTAB entry, checked AFTER the duration parses and the tick mode is confirmed -- the same 127 nohup/env/time already use.

