== tsort (7 cases) ==
  PASS a chain has one order regardless of tie-break -- tsort ... write a totally ordered list of items...
  PASS a diamond: BSD and this build tie-break differently -- Among items with no remaining predecessor, ... order is unspecified.
       hand-authored: this build scans node 0 upward at every step (tsort.s's own FINDFREE), which is exactly first-appearance order (GNU's tie-break) -- this host's own BSD tsort printed 'a c b d' for the identical input (measured live), a different, equally spec-legal order.
  PASS a self-pair token declares a lone node -- "a a" ... a node with no edge.
  PASS an isolated pair set: two independent chains -- Among items with no remaining predecessor, ... order is unspecified.
       hand-authored: same tie-break divergence as the diamond row above: this build's scan-from-0 always lets a freshly-freed low-index node (b, freed by a) cut ahead of an always-free-from-the-start higher-index one (c) -- this host's own BSD tsort printed 'c a d b' for the identical input (measured live).
  PASS no operand reads standard input -- If no file operands are specified, ... the standard input shall be used.
  PASS an odd number of tokens is a hard refusal -- >0 An error occurred.
       hand-authored: tsort.s's own PARSE leaves PENDING set on an unpaired trailing token, exit 1, nothing printed -- matching this host's own BSD tsort ('tsort: odd data count', rc=1, no stdout, verified live) even though the diagnostic wording differs (never compared -- see this suite's own module docstring).
  PASS a cycle forces an order and exits 1 -- a cycle ... [POSIX leaves recovery behavior unspecified]
       hand-authored: tsort.s's own forced-pick rule (first still-unresolved node in table order) is fully deterministic on a 2-node mutual cycle: 'a' is forced first (breaking the b->a edge), which frees 'b'. This host's own BSD tsort printed 'b a' and exited 0 for the identical input (measured live) -- a different order AND a different exit status, so no host can oracle this row; the expectation is this build's own documented cycle contract (src/bin/tsort.s's header), matching the task brief's own GNU-exit-1 instruction.

== tty (3 cases) ==
  PASS not a tty: redirected stdin -- Otherwise, a message shall be written indicating that standard input is not connected to a terminal ... the tty utility shall use the format: "not a tty\n"
  PASS an operand is a usage error -- SYNOPSIS -- tty (no OPERANDS)
       hand-authored: no host is the oracle here: BSD tty ignores an operand (exit 1 for the non-tty stdin), GNU tty refuses it with exit 2; the XCU SYNOPSIS lists no operands, and this tree refuses one uniformly (tty.s, uname.s).
  PASS prints the controlling terminal name -- "%s\n", <terminal name>
       hand-authored: fd 0 here is the LIVE interactive console this harness types at (no stdin= on this case, nothing redirects it) -- the host oracle always gets a piped, non-tty stdin instead (check_posixutil.py's own _host_once), so the two engines cannot possibly agree on a NAME even though both implement isatty correctly. Expected value is this suite's own boot-time assumption (the interactive session lands on console 1, FD_TTY0 -> /dev/tty1 per procfile5.s's PDEV_TAB) rather than a captured host run -- if this is wrong the diff names the real value.

