== xargs (32 cases) ==
  PASS default utility is echo, batching all of stdin -- utility ... shall be echo(1p) if utility is omitted.
  PASS -n limits arguments per invocation -- -n number -- Invoke utility using as many standard input arguments as possible, up to number arguments maximum.
  PASS adjacent blanks collapse to one delimiter -- arguments in the standard input shall be delimited by ... unquoted <blank> characters.
  PASS single quotes group a blank into one argument -- single-quote characters ... escape all ... characters ... up to the matching single-quote character.
  PASS double quotes group a blank into one argument -- double-quote characters ... escape all ... characters ... up to the matching double-quote character.
  PASS an unquoted backslash escapes one blank -- a <backslash> character ... shall escape any character.
  PASS -I substitutes replstr with one input line -- -I replstr -- ... construct the argument list(s) by replacing ... occurrences of replstr ...
  PASS no stdin arguments: utility is not invoked -- If there are no ... arguments ... it is unspecified whether utility is executed
       hand-authored: POSIX leaves the no-input case unspecified; maux follows this very host's own /usr/bin/xargs (checked live, 2026-09-14): zero invocations, exit 0 -- not bare POSIX's "-r not given -> run it once" reading.
  PASS -t traces the command line to stderr -- -t -- ... each command line ... written to the standard error ... prior to invocation.
  PASS utility not found on PATH: exit 127 -- 127 -- ... utility could not be found.
       hand-authored: checked once, before any invocation, against src/liba/pathres.s's PATHRSLV -- this host's own xargs also reports 127 for an unresolvable word, but with its own diagnostic text, which this row does not compare.
  PASS -0 items end at a NUL; blanks do not end them -- -0 -- ... arguments in the standard input are delimited only by null bytes.
  PASS -E stops reading at the eofstr -- -E eofstr  Use eofstr as the logical end-of-file string.
  PASS -E with no match reads everything -- -E eofstr  Use eofstr as the logical end-of-file string.
  PASS -L limits lines per invocation -- -L number  Call the utility ... for each non-empty number lines of arguments from standard input.
  PASS -p (prompt mode) an n declines the invocation -- -p -- Prompt mode ... utility ... at each invocation.
  PASS -r is accepted (no-run-if-empty is the behaviour) -- -r  If there are no arguments, do not run the utility.
  PASS -x with no -n/-s is refused, as the host refuses it -- -x  Terminate if a constructed argument list will not fit.
  PASS -0 quotes and a backslash are ordinary bytes -- -0 -- ...arguments ... are delimited only by null bytes. Any <blank>, <newline>, quote or <backslash> is literal.
  PASS -0 -n 1 makes one invocation per item -- -n number -- Invoke utility using as many standard input arguments as possible, up to number.
  PASS -0 a last item with no NUL is still an item -- -0 -- ...arguments in the standard input are delimited only by null bytes.
  PASS -0 a tab inside an item is data -- -0 -- Any <blank> is literal.
  PASS -0 a newline inside an item is data, not a delimiter -- -0 -- Any <newline> is literal.
       hand-authored: host-oracled to the byte on both hosts ('a\nb c' printed as two rows, 'a' and 'b c'), but maux_lines() splits a file on CR only, so an LF INSIDE one printed argument stays inside one row on this side. The expectation is the host's bytes under maux's line convention: ONE invocation (-n 2 took both items) whose first argument holds the LF. A tokenizer that broke at the newline would print 'a b' then 'c'.
  PASS -0 a NUL as the last byte of a read chunk -- -0 -- ...arguments in the standard input are delimited only by null bytes.
  PASS -0 a NUL as the first byte of the next read chunk -- -0 -- ...arguments in the standard input are delimited only by null bytes.
  PASS -0 with -I: a NUL ends the line, nothing is stripped -- -I replstr ... -0 -- ...arguments ... delimited only by null bytes.
  PASS -p a y runs the invocation -- -p -- ...the user is prompted; an affirmative response causes the invocation.
  PASS -p asks once per invocation and each answer counts -- -p -- ...prompted ... at each invocation.
  PASS -p Y and a word that begins with y are affirmative -- -p -- ...an affirmative response ...
  PASS -p an empty reply declines -- -p -- ...skip the invocation unless affirmative.
  PASS -p the prompt is the whole command line -- -p -- ...the utility name and arguments ... written to stderr.
  PASS -p with -I prompts for each substituted line -- -p -- ... -I replstr ...
  PASS -p with -0 prompts for NUL-separated items -- -p -- ... -0 ...

