[check-posixutil] KNOWN_GAPS:
  expr/':' a malformed BRE is a usage error (exit 2) on the hosts: regex.s has no compile step (the Fowler/Spencer report counts 28 regcomp-error rows 'reported but unscored' for the same reason): a malformed BRE is a SAFE NO-MATCH, so maux prints an empty line and exits 1 where BSD ('parentheses not balanced') and GNU ('Unmatched ( or \(') both exit 2. The same holds for an unterminated [. Giving the engine a validating pass is a change to the shared library, not to expr.
  grep/-E extended regular expressions is unimplemented: grep.s's GETOPT table (GREPOPTS/the option switch in START) has no 'E' case at all -- 'E' falls through to :usage, exit 2. BSD grep -E treats '+' as one-or-more and matches, exit 0.
  mkdir/-m mode is unimplemented: mkdir.s's OPTLIST is the single byte "p" (grep OPTLIST in src/bin/mkdir.s) -- '-m' is not a registered option at all, so GETOPT reports it bad and mkdir.s takes the :usage exit-2 path before ever reaching a dir operand. BSD mkdir -m 755 creates the directory with that mode and exits 0.
  sort/sortk -k2,2d dictionary order is not implemented: sort.s accepts the key types b f n r; d and i are refused as a usage error (exit 2). Host exit 0.
  sort/sortk -k2,2i ignore-nonprinting is not implemented: sort.s accepts the key types b f n r; d and i are refused as a usage error (exit 2). Host exit 0.
  sort/sortk -k1.2 character offsets are not implemented: PARSEKEY reads field numbers only; '.' after the number is junk and the usage-error exit 2 is taken. Not part of the per-key-modifier brief; recorded so the divergence is gated.
  find/-perm has no ProDOS meaning (held red, permanent): PERMANENT. ProDOS keeps a destroy/rename/backup/write/read access byte, not rwx for a user, a group and the world: there is no honest octal mapping to match a mode against (docs/ANACHRONISMS.md sec W2 rules a Unix rwx mapping out). -perm is not a row in findexpr.s's flag table, so TOKENIZE falls through to TOKBADFLG, exit 2; BSD and GNU find accept it and exit 0.
  find/-user has no ProDOS meaning (held red, permanent): PERMANENT. ProDOS files have no owner and this machine has no user database, so there is no name to resolve and nothing to compare it with. -user falls through TOKENIZE to TOKBADFLG, exit 2; BSD and GNU find accept it.
  find/-group has no ProDOS meaning (held red, permanent): PERMANENT. As -user: ProDOS files have no group and this machine has no group database. -group falls through TOKENIZE to TOKBADFLG, exit 2; the hosts accept it (BSD exit 0, GNU exit 1 when the group does not exist -- neither is 2).
  printf/%e is unimplemented (no floating point): This machine has no floating point (src/bin/printf.s's own header); %e falls through to the unknown-conversion diagnostic + BADNUM, unlike a host printf which formats a real float.
